WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.308
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4524.

2
00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:16.615
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:27.692
<v GEORGE BRAXTON>What’s the day today?

4
00:00:27.692 --> 00:00:36.000
<v STEPHANIE DRAGOON>September ninth.

5
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.744
<v VELMA CARHEEL>How long have you all been here and not eating boudin? [Laughs]

6
00:00:40.744 --> 00:00:42.326
<v DRAGOON>[Laughs] About two years.

7
00:00:42.326 --> 00:00:43.116
<v REBECCA COOPER>Two years?

8
00:00:43.116 --> 00:00:44.302
Almost three now.

9
00:00:44.302 --> 00:00:45.488
<v CARHEEL>That’s not bad.

10
00:00:45.488 --> 00:00:53.000
Good, real good. [Laughs] Most people, that’s the first thing they want to try when they get here.

11
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.955
<v COOPER>When we first moved here, we both ate seafood, though, so we had like shrimp every day.

12
00:00:59.955 --> 00:01:01.182
<v DRAGOON>Po’boys, shrimp po’boys.

13
00:01:01.182 --> 00:01:02.409
<v CARHEEL>Oh, yeah. [Laughs]

14
00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:02.818
<v DRAGOON>Catfish.

15
00:01:02.818 --> 00:01:03.636
<v COOPER>Cajun seafood.

16
00:01:03.636 --> 00:01:08.136
This one that’s like right around the corner from our house.

17
00:01:08.136 --> 00:01:09.364
<v DRAGOON>It’s so good.

18
00:01:09.364 --> 00:01:11.000
We miss it.

19
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.353
<v COOPER>Now I get a po’boy with French fries in it and I pretend it’s shrimp.

20
00:01:17.353 --> 00:01:20.741
I just close my eyes and pretend. [Laughs]

21
00:01:20.741 --> 00:01:22.435
<v CARHEEL>So what’s in it?

22
00:01:22.435 --> 00:01:24.976
<v COOPER>French fries, cheese . . . it’s fully dressed.

23
00:01:24.976 --> 00:01:30.059
Everything’s the same, but I just put French fries instead of shrimp.

24
00:01:30.059 --> 00:01:30.906
<v CARHEEL>Really? [Laughs]

25
00:01:30.906 --> 00:01:32.600
<v COOPER>It’s not the same.

26
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:37.259
<v DRAGOON>Well some places do like grill or fried like vegetables instead.

27
00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:37.682
<v CARHEEL>Yeah.

28
00:01:37.682 --> 00:01:38.106
Okay.

29
00:01:38.106 --> 00:01:44.459
<v DRAGOON>I like to start with my little spiel, and then we’ll go into some questions.

30
00:01:44.459 --> 00:01:47.000
You going to start over?

31
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:47.398
Okay.

32
00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:49.390
Today is September ninth, 2015.

33
00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.374
We’re at the home of Velma Carheel in Westlake, Louisiana.

34
00:01:53.374 --> 00:02:00.545
I’m Stephanie Dragoon with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

35
00:02:00.545 --> 00:02:07.715
I’m here today with Mr. George Braxton and Ms. Velma Carheel to talk about memories of Mossville, Louisiana,

36
00:02:07.715 --> 00:02:15.285
as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

37
00:02:15.285 --> 00:02:18.073
Thank you so much for your time.

38
00:02:18.073 --> 00:02:20.463
This is our second session together.

39
00:02:20.463 --> 00:02:23.650
The first one was about a month ago.

40
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:31.618
We’re just going to continue with some questions about your family history and was wondering when your family moved here.

41
00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:34.008
When did your family move here?

42
00:02:34.008 --> 00:02:34.407
<v BRAXTON>Well . . .

43
00:02:34.407 --> 00:02:36.000
<v DRAGOON>Or to Mossville?

44
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.726
<v BRAXTON>The earliest that I’m aware of is the mid-1800s.

45
00:02:42.726 --> 00:02:56.926
As a point of reference, like I said, my great-grandfather started Mount Zion Baptist Church in Mossville in 1866.

46
00:02:56.926 --> 00:03:03.653
Exactly how long before that, I’m not quite sure,

47
00:03:03.653 --> 00:03:21.589
except to say that I discovered the records where he was on the . . . This is my grandfather on my father’s side of the family.

48
00:03:21.589 --> 00:03:41.021
He was a slave on the manifest of the Orleans coming into the port of New Orleans [Louisiana] in 1840 . . . so somewhere between 1840 and 1866.

49
00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:47.000
<v DRAGOON>And how did you find that information?

50
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.861
<v BRAXTON>I do ancestry research.

51
00:03:49.861 --> 00:03:50.576
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

52
00:03:50.576 --> 00:03:55.582
Do you know how he found Mossville?

53
00:03:55.582 --> 00:04:06.310
<v BRAXTON>No. That’s what I’m trying to fill in the blanks between the 1840 and 1866.

54
00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:19.184
In fact, I’m actually fixing to go and do some research in Virginia before 1840 on that end.

55
00:04:19.184 --> 00:04:30.627
In fact, I planned to be gone about this week, roughly . . . so real quick, I’m going.

56
00:04:30.627 --> 00:04:34.203
So that, I don't know.

57
00:04:34.203 --> 00:04:52.082
Now on my mother’s side of the family, her family moved to Mossville, I believe, around the 1930 timeframe or so, somewhere in that area.

58
00:04:52.082 --> 00:05:11.392
My mother was born in 1916 and I recall her saying that some of her younger sisters and brothers were born at the time when they moved.

59
00:05:11.392 --> 00:05:14.253
She remembered a move.

60
00:05:14.253 --> 00:05:18.544
<v DRAGOON>And where did she move from?

61
00:05:18.544 --> 00:05:22.120
<v BRAXTON>Sugartown, up near Deridder, Louisiana.

62
00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:34.278
So it was probably somewhere in the ‘30s . . . I mean, 1916 . . . in the ‘20s or the ‘30s.

63
00:05:34.278 --> 00:05:40.000
<v DRAGOON>Did she ever say why they moved?

64
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.875
<v BRAXTON>No. I really don’t know.

65
00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:52.075
I do know that in 1896, they had an eighty-acre land grant up in that area.

66
00:05:52.075 --> 00:06:03.575
His father, my grandfather on my mother’s side . . . my great-grandfather on my mother’s side had a land grant up there.

67
00:06:03.575 --> 00:06:06.450
He passed in the ‘30s.

68
00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:13.925
I think at some point after that is when they moved down here.

69
00:06:13.925 --> 00:06:17.375
<v DRAGOON>Did she move with family members?

70
00:06:17.375 --> 00:06:17.950
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

71
00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:21.400
<v DRAGOON>Did she move with family members?

72
00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.700
<v BRAXTON>The whole family moved.

73
00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.000
<v DRAGOON>The whole family.

74
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.089
<v BRAXTON>The whole family moved.

75
00:06:29.089 --> 00:06:44.532
In fact, I recall her saying it took over two weeks to move . . . and that’s only like fifty, sixty miles.

76
00:06:44.532 --> 00:06:55.342
That was a big move back in them days . . . by horse and buggy. [Laughs]

77
00:06:55.342 --> 00:06:56.114
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

78
00:06:56.114 --> 00:06:57.658
Let’s see.

79
00:06:57.658 --> 00:07:04.608
Did anyone in your family serve in the military?

80
00:07:04.608 --> 00:07:08.468
<v BRAXTON>None of my grandparents served.

81
00:07:08.468 --> 00:07:26.228
But I have a whole host of uncles on both sides of the family that served in the military from World War I,

82
00:07:26.228 --> 00:07:39.354
World War II, and the Korean Conflict, as well as the latter, the Vietnam conflict as well.

83
00:07:39.354 --> 00:07:59.430
Some of my daddy’s brothers served in the military in World War I. I know at least several of my uncles did on my father’s side:

84
00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:03.291
Rogers Braxton and Melvin Braxton.

85
00:08:03.291 --> 00:08:19.506
On my mother’s side, Frederick Towner, for sure, and possibly some others . . . a whole host of other relatives, cousins and such.

86
00:08:19.506 --> 00:08:28.000
<v DRAGOON>Do you know what branch of service they were in?

87
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.955
<v BRAXTON>I think it was all army.

88
00:08:31.955 --> 00:08:33.932
That’s my recollection.

89
00:08:33.932 --> 00:08:41.841
<v DRAGOON>Did they ever talk about their experiences in the army with you?

90
00:08:41.841 --> 00:08:43.159
<v BRAXTON>Never did.

91
00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:44.477
Never did.

92
00:08:44.477 --> 00:08:57.000
<v DRAGOON>Were there stories that your uncles talked about, like World War II, around the family . . . like family stories?

93
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:02.588
<v BRAXTON>No. Only one, and it really was not about the war itself.

94
00:09:02.588 --> 00:09:10.505
It was just a story that . . . about forty years ago that my father’s brother told me about,

95
00:09:10.505 --> 00:09:19.353
when they went to . . . it was either to enlist or to sign up to be on the draft roll.

96
00:09:19.353 --> 00:09:35.186
Both of them were born in the latter 1800s at the time they signed up for World War I. They were at some gathering point where all the people were gathering to signed up.

97
00:09:35.186 --> 00:09:44.966
The uncle that’s telling me the story was the youngest of the two, Melvin, and he was with his brother, Rogers.

98
00:09:44.966 --> 00:09:52.417
Rogers was sort of a timid fellow, even though he was the biggest and the oldest.

99
00:09:52.417 --> 00:10:02.662
Some guys kept picking up on him . . . picking at him, and he just, the younger one just couldn’t stand it no more.

100
00:10:02.662 --> 00:10:09.181
They wasn’t picking at the younger one, they was picking at the older one.

101
00:10:09.181 --> 00:10:13.373
He said he just couldn’t stand it no more,

102
00:10:13.373 --> 00:10:23.618
and he commenced to whip about three of them. [Laughs] And his brother just kind of stood by and watched the action.

103
00:10:23.618 --> 00:10:25.946
That was just his nature.

104
00:10:25.946 --> 00:10:29.206
He just was passive in that way.

105
00:10:29.206 --> 00:10:32.000
<v DRAGOON>And that was your uncle?

106
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:42.821
<v BRAXTON>That was my uncle Melvin telling me the story about . . . and he told me the story to tell me how reactive he was in his younger days to things,

107
00:10:42.821 --> 00:10:45.433
and how he had changed over time.

108
00:10:45.433 --> 00:10:49.164
That was his whole point in telling me the story.

109
00:10:49.164 --> 00:10:51.776
<v DRAGOON>And now he’s more laid back? [Laughs]

110
00:10:51.776 --> 00:10:53.642
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, he died in 1982.

111
00:10:53.642 --> 00:10:54.388
<v DRAGOON>Oh, yeah.

112
00:10:54.388 --> 00:10:57.000
When he told you the story.

113
00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.589
<v BRAXTON>But he had mellowed extremely.

114
00:10:59.589 --> 00:11:01.143
He was married.

115
00:11:01.143 --> 00:11:06.839
He was actually a very gentle person, a very gentle person.

116
00:11:06.839 --> 00:11:16.161
I remember in 1982 I was visiting him and he was in his mid-eighties and he was married.

117
00:11:16.161 --> 00:11:20.304
His wife was much, much taller than him.

118
00:11:20.304 --> 00:11:26.518
I remember she came and called us in for lunch one day.

119
00:11:26.518 --> 00:11:33.250
She had this big spread on the table of . . . I’ll never forget it.

120
00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:38.429
It was cabbage, and meatloaf, and a host of vegetables.

121
00:11:38.429 --> 00:11:43.607
It was like total of like three meats or so.

122
00:11:43.607 --> 00:11:50.339
She always . . . Just for the two of them, she fixed these huge meals.

123
00:11:50.339 --> 00:11:59.143
When he went in, he glanced at the table and he said, “Baby, that sure smell good.”

124
00:11:59.143 --> 00:12:08.464
As he was kind of just walking by at the narrow walkway between the table and something else,

125
00:12:08.464 --> 00:12:21.929
he turned and he tiptoed and he kissed her, and then he slapped her on the butt. [Laughs] I say, “Go, Uncle!” [Laughs] That was him.

126
00:12:21.929 --> 00:12:24.000
That’s the way.

127
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:24.587
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

128
00:12:24.587 --> 00:12:29.280
So I’m going to backtrack a little bit.

129
00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.973
Last time we talked about your father’s business.

130
00:12:33.973 --> 00:12:48.053
I was just wondering, could you tell me a little bit more about the garbage collection business and how he came to start it,

131
00:12:48.053 --> 00:12:52.747
and who it served . . . the route, I guess?

132
00:12:52.747 --> 00:12:58.027
<v BRAXTON>Exactly how he got started, I’m not absolutely sure.

133
00:12:58.027 --> 00:13:01.547
But I’m sure that you know,

134
00:13:01.547 --> 00:13:23.253
he probably started picking up from a couple of places by some happenstance and it just continued to grow until he had basically the garbage collection business for the area that we now call Maplewood and Hollywood.

135
00:13:23.253 --> 00:13:37.333
And we kept that business basically till the latter ‘60s, when it became incorporated into the city of Sulphur [Louisiana] for the most part.

136
00:13:37.333 --> 00:13:45.547
After the time that my father had passed, my mother had continued operating it.

137
00:13:45.547 --> 00:13:52.000
That’s kind of the sum story of it, I believe.

138
00:13:52.000 --> 00:14:00.783
<v DRAGOON>What was the reception to your mother taking over the business?

139
00:14:00.783 --> 00:14:03.977
<v BRAXTON>Hostile, in a sense.

140
00:14:03.977 --> 00:14:19.147
Even though the business being what it was, women in the mid-60s, 1963, you didn’t see women with businesses,

141
00:14:19.147 --> 00:14:33.519
particularly black women, and particularly a business that catered to a wide area of the public like that.

142
00:14:33.519 --> 00:14:48.690
There were a lot of attempts to try to take the business . . . take customers and such by various people.

143
00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:58.271
She went through the process of getting a franchise for those areas.

144
00:14:58.271 --> 00:15:03.860
It was a tough business to maintain.

145
00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:11.047
Finally, I can’t say exactly what the reason was,

146
00:15:11.047 --> 00:15:35.000
but it was a combination of things—the city incorporating parts of the area and everything that just made it a business that just didn’t work anymore at that point.

147
00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.378
<v DRAGOON>And you said that was in the ‘60s?

148
00:15:37.378 --> 00:15:40.054
<v BRAXTON>That was starting when my father died in 1963.

149
00:15:40.054 --> 00:15:41.243
<v DRAGOON>Oh, when he died.

150
00:15:41.243 --> 00:15:42.730
<v BRAXTON>That’s when she took over.

151
00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:43.027
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

152
00:15:43.027 --> 00:15:46.000
So she had it for a handful of years?

153
00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.005
<v BRAXTON>I’m sorry?

154
00:15:47.005 --> 00:15:51.025
<v DRAGOON>Was she operating it for a few years?

155
00:15:51.025 --> 00:15:51.527
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

156
00:15:51.527 --> 00:15:53.035
After his death.

157
00:15:53.035 --> 00:15:54.040
<v DRAGOON>I see.

158
00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:55.547
<v BRAXTON>Before his death.

159
00:15:55.547 --> 00:16:00.572
It was after he passed, it became much more formalized.

160
00:16:00.572 --> 00:16:09.617
She had bookkeepers, and went through all the various legal things . . . a business license and blah, blah, blah.

161
00:16:09.617 --> 00:16:15.144
It was much more formalized than the way he operated it.

162
00:16:15.144 --> 00:16:24.189
In fact, I still have all the checkbooks and stuff, the various things that she used checks for.

163
00:16:24.189 --> 00:16:37.254
For the most part, when my father operated it, for I can’t say exactly how many years he did, but it was before I was born.

164
00:16:37.254 --> 00:16:37.756
He

165
00:16:37.756 --> 00:16:43.284
<c.p>had relatives working for him: a brother and a first cousin.

166
00:16:43.284 --> 00:16:52.831
Robert Braxton, a brother, was one of the last ones that worked for him, and a cousin named Tolbert.

167
00:16:52.831 --> 00:17:03.383
It was a whole host of other people, actually from the Mossville area, that from time to time worked for her.

168
00:17:03.383 --> 00:17:12.428
In fact, I talked with several people recently that had worked for her, that were guys about seven,

169
00:17:12.428 --> 00:17:22.478
eight years older than me at that point in time, they started working for her, after my father passed on.

170
00:17:22.478 --> 00:17:27.000
<v DRAGOON>What did they say about working for her?

171
00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.809
<v BRAXTON>Actually, they spoke very highly of that.

172
00:17:29.809 --> 00:17:37.836
But the job, you have to understand, the job was not a great job, collecting garbage back in them days.

173
00:17:37.836 --> 00:17:43.455
You actually, at the end of the day, you had garbage all over you.

174
00:17:43.455 --> 00:17:45.060
You smelled like garbage.

175
00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:54.291
One day . . . In fact, the guy that I last talked to, he worked one day. [Laughs] I was basically raised up with him.

176
00:17:54.291 --> 00:18:05.528
They used to take . . . they used to have the old metal garbage cans and they would take a full garbage can that had been sitting for a week,

177
00:18:05.528 --> 00:18:09.943
ripe, and they would dump it into a number three washtub.

178
00:18:09.943 --> 00:18:13.555
I don't know if you know what that is.

179
00:18:13.555 --> 00:18:22.786
It’s a tub about that tall and about that big around and that’s the tubs that, basically, people, they call it a washtub.

180
00:18:22.786 --> 00:18:28.405
People used to wash clothes in it . . . used to take a bath in it.

181
00:18:28.405 --> 00:18:32.819
That’s the tubs that we bathed in when we was kids.

182
00:18:32.819 --> 00:18:35.629
We didn’t have a bathtub as such.

183
00:18:35.629 --> 00:18:40.445
And they would dump those into . . . those garbage cans into those tubs.

184
00:18:40.445 --> 00:18:51.281
Then they would hoist it up on their shoulder and they would hold one handle and they would . . . the side of the truck was above their head,

185
00:18:51.281 --> 00:18:54.492
so they would dump it over the side.

186
00:18:54.492 --> 00:19:00.913
This guy in particular, his name’s Joe [Morrow?], was telling me about when he did that.

187
00:19:00.913 --> 00:19:09.742
One time he was working that day with one of my uncles and some of the liquid and the maggots came down

188
00:19:09.742 --> 00:19:10.545
<c.p>his arm.

189
00:19:10.545 --> 00:19:12.151
That was very typical.

190
00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:20.980
And he said . . . my uncle told him, he said, “Oh, you get used to it.” [Laughs] But that was his last day.

191
00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:24.592
That was Joe’s last day on the job. [Laughs]

192
00:19:24.592 --> 00:19:27.000
<v DRAGOON>His first and last. [Laughs]

193
00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.885
<v BRAXTON>First and last.

194
00:19:28.885 --> 00:19:34.538
But others worked for long, long periods of time.

195
00:19:34.538 --> 00:19:39.564
That was just Joe’s reaction to his experience.

196
00:19:39.564 --> 00:19:42.705
Others just dealt with it.

197
00:19:42.705 --> 00:19:54.013
When I was a kid, before my father died, I used to go to work with him sometimes.

198
00:19:54.013 --> 00:19:57.154
That was quite an experience.

199
00:19:57.154 --> 00:20:10.346
The neighborhoods that we . . . In fact, the entire one hundred percent of the client list, none of that was in Mossville.

200
00:20:10.346 --> 00:20:20.397
That was all outside of Mossville, and it was all totally one hundred percent white communities.

201
00:20:20.397 --> 00:20:24.795
We used to stop at various places.

202
00:20:24.795 --> 00:20:34.218
That’s my biggest memory is some of the places we stopped at to eat lunch.

203
00:20:34.218 --> 00:20:58.090
We would stop . . . and I pass by this place all the time to this day where we used to stop at under an oak tree and we would buy chocolate milk and a honey bun . . . sardines and crackers,

204
00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:01.231
with something they called all-sausage.

205
00:21:01.231 --> 00:21:05.000
Y’all probably never heard of it.

206
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:13.795
It’s sausage in a can and it’s packed in pure oil, like vegetable oil.

207
00:21:13.795 --> 00:21:25.731
It was terrible. [Laughs] It was terrible! [Laughs] We used to buy salami and bologna and bread and liverwurst.

208
00:21:25.731 --> 00:21:28.872
You know what liverwurst is?

209
00:21:28.872 --> 00:21:31.385
We would buy liverwurst.

210
00:21:31.385 --> 00:21:38.923
Liverwurst was like a liver pate with a fat ring around it.

211
00:21:38.923 --> 00:21:42.064
So I had those experiences.

212
00:21:42.064 --> 00:21:54.000
That’s stuff I didn’t eat at home, so eating that out on the road was quite an experience.

213
00:21:54.000 --> 00:22:00.938
Then, every once in a while— see, this was still during the time of segregation,

214
00:22:00.938 --> 00:22:12.038
so to speak—there was one café that was on Highway 90 that we would go to every once in a while and buy hamburgers.

215
00:22:12.038 --> 00:22:18.050
It was basically kind of right next to the Mossville community, this place.

216
00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:26.838
It wasn’t in Mossville, but it was right on . . . kind of on the line where it was located at,

217
00:22:26.838 --> 00:22:28.688
<c.p>and buy hamburgers, cheeseburgers.

218
00:22:28.688 --> 00:22:35.162
This was basically a white establishment, so we used to go to the back.

219
00:22:35.162 --> 00:22:38.400
We had to go to the back,

220
00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:47.188
and there was a back room that was . . . the décor was quite different from the front of the place.

221
00:22:47.188 --> 00:22:58.750
I recall that I used to sit in there and they had a swinging door that went from where we were at to the front.

222
00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:08.000
The front was well-lit with red and white checkered tablecloths, and it was windows, so it was very, very bright.

223
00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:16.325
The room we were in had no windows and it had a screen door, and no air conditioning.

224
00:23:16.325 --> 00:23:19.562
There was air conditioning in the front.

225
00:23:19.562 --> 00:23:26.500
It had one ceiling fan and a light hanging by a wire from the ceiling.

226
00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:36.212
And it had one or two tables in there that was just mismatched pieces like they was left over from somewhere.

227
00:23:36.212 --> 00:23:38.525
But the hamburgers were great.

228
00:23:38.525 --> 00:23:45.000
I mean, there was no . . . I think about the hamburgers to this day.

229
00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:49.632
I recall . . . see, I was somewhat younger than ten years old,

230
00:23:49.632 --> 00:23:55.947
I can’t say exactly, at that time because I was ten when my father died.

231
00:23:55.947 --> 00:24:10.263
But I recall that when the people would go back and forth between the swinging door that separated the front of the place from the back of . . . the white side from the black side,

232
00:24:10.263 --> 00:24:17.000
that you would feel that rush of cool air conditioned air would roll through the room.

233
00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:26.684
I used to . . . I never did give it much thought then about what it was like to be eating on the other side,

234
00:24:26.684 --> 00:24:33.000
but I was very aware that there was a big difference in the two sides.

235
00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:41.000
So that was one of the experiences that I had with him and operating that business and everything.

236
00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:45.900
It was very . . . the business was a labor-intensive business,

237
00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:55.701
even to the point of once they ended their day, they would haul the trash to a dump,

238
00:24:55.701 --> 00:24:58.968
and they had like public dumps,

239
00:24:58.968 --> 00:25:10.947
and pull it all off the truck by tying a rope to a tree stump or something and then drive it off.

240
00:25:10.947 --> 00:25:29.459
They had boards that would actually . . . that was in the truck before it was loaded that would pull the trash off because that was prior to . . . They really didn't have garbage trucks as such

241
00:25:29.459 --> 00:25:35.448
<c.p>around here like we have now, all automated and so forth.

242
00:25:35.448 --> 00:25:44.160
For some reasons at periods of time, he would dump on property that was our property.

243
00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:54.505
He would dump this stuff and you could imagine, day after day of huge truckloads, a lot of trash.

244
00:25:54.505 --> 00:26:00.495
So in hauling this garbage, he also hauled more than garbage.

245
00:26:00.495 --> 00:26:05.940
It was like other trash: old leftover furniture, washing machines,

246
00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:18.463
and all kind of various metal stuff that he also saved to try to reclaim the metals and stuff from it to sell,

247
00:26:18.463 --> 00:26:22.819
like copper, brass, aluminum, steel, and so forth.

248
00:26:22.819 --> 00:26:38.064
So that was kind of an offshoot of just the collection business to make the whole thing work was to reclaim some of this stuff and sell it.

249
00:26:38.064 --> 00:26:45.142
I can remember even selling . . . we used to go sell mattress, the cotton.

250
00:26:45.142 --> 00:26:50.587
See, cotton mattresses used to be made of just cotton.

251
00:26:50.587 --> 00:26:53.310
We would sell the cotton.

252
00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:57.121
We would sell newspapers . . . collect them . . . magazines.

253
00:26:57.121 --> 00:27:02.021
Everything had a recycle value to a great extent.

254
00:27:02.021 --> 00:27:06.377
I came up experiencing that during that time.

255
00:27:06.377 --> 00:27:14.000
So that was kind of an overview of what that business was like.

256
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:22.294
<v DRAGOON>I just want to go back a little bit about your experience in the café.

257
00:27:22.294 --> 00:27:27.824
How did your father talk to you about the two . . .

258
00:27:27.824 --> 00:27:34.459
<v BRAXTON>I don’t . . . I mean, he may have, but I don’t recall that.

259
00:27:34.459 --> 00:27:41.094
That’s why I say I was aware that there was a difference.

260
00:27:41.094 --> 00:27:48.282
But as I grew up, my parents never, ever taught me to discriminate.

261
00:27:48.282 --> 00:27:48.835
Never.

262
00:27:48.835 --> 00:27:52.153
Actually, it was quite the opposite.

263
00:27:52.153 --> 00:27:58.235
My personal experience was the opposite from a lot of people.

264
00:27:58.235 --> 00:28:09.294
There were a lot of people, white people, that were . . . that helped my family before and after my father’s death.

265
00:28:09.294 --> 00:28:17.588
One of the things I recall that was not a typical thing in that day,

266
00:28:17.588 --> 00:28:25.882
and especially Mossville, if your experience was Mossville, Mossville was a completely totally black community.

267
00:28:25.882 --> 00:28:32.518
I recall when my father died, his funeral, I remember I was

268
00:28:32.518 --> 00:28:40.259
<c.p>like impressed, surprised, highly aware there were so many white people at his funeral.

269
00:28:40.259 --> 00:28:48.000
So I don't know if that kind of answered your question or not.

270
00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:54.842
<v DRAGOON>And those are people from the neighborhoods he was operating his business in?

271
00:28:54.842 --> 00:28:56.947
<v BRAXTON>What, at his funeral?

272
00:28:56.947 --> 00:28:58.000
<v DRAGOON>Yes.

273
00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.200
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, yeah.

274
00:28:59.200 --> 00:29:10.000
The people that he associated with in his business life and personal life, to some extent, I guess.

275
00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:20.200
I can remember that there were several members of the ministry that were there that were white.

276
00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:32.200
I kind of remember them because we had contact with them for some period after my father’s death. [phone rings]

277
00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.000
<v CARHEEL>Your ringer’s on?

278
00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:34.600
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

279
00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:36.400
<v CARHEEL>Your ringer’s on?

280
00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:37.000
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

281
00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:39.400
<v CARHEEL>Your ringer is on?

282
00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:47.800
<v BRAXTON>No. My phone will ring for certain things even if the ringer is off.

283
00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:52.000
I have it set that way.

284
00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:59.091
<v DRAGOON>And you also mentioned that your mother was met with some hostility when she took over the business?

285
00:29:59.091 --> 00:29:59.485
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

286
00:29:59.485 --> 00:30:05.000
<v DRAGOON>I was wondering if you could talk a little bit more about that.

287
00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:17.833
<v BRAXTON>Well basically, I think it was simply because she was a woman and she was seen as being, I guess, weak, vulnerable,

288
00:30:17.833 --> 00:30:26.000
in the sense that . . . and there was nothing protecting the area of the business,

289
00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:33.000
that areas that she had in at the time to start with.

290
00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:38.833
And kind of that’s what her franchise was all about.

291
00:30:38.833 --> 00:30:44.667
That’s why I mentioned earlier that after my father died,

292
00:30:44.667 --> 00:30:58.083
soon began a pursuit to get a franchise to protect the area from encroachment by other people . . . just [canvasing?] is kind of like,

293
00:30:58.083 --> 00:31:01.000
no difference than businesses today.

294
00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:10.333
I mean, they have Popeye’s [Louisiana Kitchen] and they have Church’s and they have Kentucky Fried,

295
00:31:10.333 --> 00:31:19.083
and they all trying to get your business in one way or another through advertising.

296
00:31:19.083 --> 00:31:23.167
Of course, this was a service business.

297
00:31:23.167 --> 00:31:31.333
So it was . . . There were people that we knew from my own community that,

298
00:31:31.333 --> 00:31:41.833
in those days, doing odd jobs and such was a way of life for a lot of people.

299
00:31:41.833 --> 00:31:57.000
Some of them that was doing very similar odd jobs, they tried to capitalize on the fact that my father had passed and it was this

300
00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:08.083
<c.p>area out there where he had this business and they may be able to pick up some of it.

301
00:32:08.083 --> 00:32:11.000
It’s just that simple.

302
00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:18.637
<v DRAGOON>How did life change for your family after your father passed?

303
00:32:18.637 --> 00:32:21.414
<v BRAXTON>Oh, it was rough.

304
00:32:21.414 --> 00:32:31.828
My mother didn’t work prior to my father’s death, so he was the sole breadwinner.

305
00:32:31.828 --> 00:32:49.879
So after he passed, between trying to maintain the business and trying to draw Social Security from his prior work life, that’s how we made it.

306
00:32:49.879 --> 00:33:02.376
It was some rough times there in terms of things like . . . did not have electricity for a while,

307
00:33:02.376 --> 00:33:11.401
but there was some . . . even though there were some good things that happened.

308
00:33:11.401 --> 00:33:24.592
We got, afterwards, before my father died, we had a deep water well, but we had no indoor plumbing.

309
00:33:24.592 --> 00:33:32.924
We got indoor plumbing and we got bathroom plumbing in the bathroom.

310
00:33:32.924 --> 00:33:41.255
We had plumbing like with the kitchen and we had hot water.

311
00:33:41.255 --> 00:33:44.032
We had washing machines.

312
00:33:44.032 --> 00:33:48.197
But we did get indoor plumbing.

313
00:33:48.197 --> 00:33:54.446
In the bathroom, that was a big, big deal.

314
00:33:54.446 --> 00:34:00.000
<v DRAGOON>What year was that, do you think?

315
00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:06.402
<v BRAXTON>It was in the latter ‘60s, around 1966 or thereabouts.

316
00:34:06.402 --> 00:34:16.005
But the thing was, that even though we had periods where we didn’t have electricity,

317
00:34:16.005 --> 00:34:26.888
and we had commodities was a big thing that a lot of people received in those days.

318
00:34:26.888 --> 00:34:28.808
<v DRAGOON>What was that?

319
00:34:28.808 --> 00:34:29.449
<v BRAXTON>Commodities.

320
00:34:29.449 --> 00:34:42.252
It was a food, government-sponsored food supplements: canned beef, canned pork, cheeses, rice . . . a lot of staple items like that.

321
00:34:42.252 --> 00:34:53.776
We used to get that, but through all of that, that was no sad times in my life.

322
00:34:53.776 --> 00:34:58.257
It was all very happy and joyful.

323
00:34:58.257 --> 00:35:12.341
Even without electricity, I can remember the nights, you know, that we spent with kerosene lamps and such doing homework and everything.

324
00:35:12.341 --> 00:35:16.822
My mother always made it feel comfortable.

325
00:35:16.822 --> 00:35:20.664
There never was a bad experience.

326
00:35:20.664 --> 00:35:30.907
I’m sure that as an adult, she had her stresses, but it didn’t come to us.

327
00:35:30.907 --> 00:35:34.107
And we ate very well.

328
00:35:34.107 --> 00:35:39.869
Food was a thing that I looked forward to.

329
00:35:39.869 --> 00:35:43.710
We used to raise a garden.

330
00:35:43.710 --> 00:36:00.355
Particularly, my mother and I would plant strawberries and peppers and pole beans . . . just greens . . . a whole lot of things we would raise in the garden.

331
00:36:00.355 --> 00:36:09.958
It just kind of . . . it wasn’t really a garden that the family could survive on,

332
00:36:09.958 --> 00:36:17.000
but it supplemented other things that we had to eat.

333
00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:22.188
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever share your produce with other families?

334
00:36:22.188 --> 00:36:23.918
<v BRAXTON>No. Not really.

335
00:36:23.918 --> 00:36:26.224
It wasn’t that big.

336
00:36:26.224 --> 00:36:31.412
<v DRAGOON>And you tended the garden with your mother together?

337
00:36:31.412 --> 00:36:32.565
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah.

338
00:36:32.565 --> 00:36:33.141
Absolutely.

339
00:36:33.141 --> 00:36:37.753
<v DRAGOON>Did you have any plants for medicinal uses?

340
00:36:37.753 --> 00:36:38.329
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

341
00:36:38.329 --> 00:36:51.012
That was a common thing that people would . . . my mother used to like plant mint by the front porch all the time.

342
00:36:51.012 --> 00:36:59.659
We used to do like do things like many times we made pine straw tea.

343
00:36:59.659 --> 00:37:00.812
<v DRAGOON>What’s that?

344
00:37:00.812 --> 00:37:04.847
<v BRAXTON>You know what a pine tree is?

345
00:37:04.847 --> 00:37:06.000
<v DRAGOON>Yes.

346
00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:14.571
<v BRAXTON>You would take those green straws off a pine tree, just wash them like you would wash greens or something.

347
00:37:14.571 --> 00:37:22.286
Then you would take them, put them in a pot of boiling water, and you would boil them.

348
00:37:22.286 --> 00:37:27.000
After you boil it, the piney flavor would saturate the water.

349
00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:32.143
At that point, you would treat it more or less like lemonade.

350
00:37:32.143 --> 00:37:33.857
It looked like lemonade.

351
00:37:33.857 --> 00:37:38.143
And you would pour the juice off in a glass.

352
00:37:38.143 --> 00:37:46.714
You could drink it hot or you could drink it cold, put sugar in it or maybe squeeze the lemon.

353
00:37:46.714 --> 00:37:54.857
I made some here a while back just for the heck of it, so the kids could taste it.

354
00:37:54.857 --> 00:38:03.000
They also . . . What was popular back at the time was turpentine, which was also made from a pine tree.

355
00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:04.714
That was commercially produced.

356
00:38:04.714 --> 00:38:08.143
We used to take turpentine with various things.

357
00:38:08.143 --> 00:38:16.714
I can remember one time we had this old wringer type washing machine, which you all probably haven’t seen before.

358
00:38:16.714 --> 00:38:18.429
It’s the open-top machine.

359
00:38:18.429 --> 00:38:21.000
It’s got an agitator in it.

360
00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:33.429
But all it do is wash and then you would have to take the clothes out of the water and put them through a crank wringer to wring them.

361
00:38:33.429 --> 00:38:38.143
Then, after you had them all wrung through the wringer, partially

362
00:38:38.143 --> 00:38:42.000
<c.p>removing the water, after you had them all out,

363
00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:51.857
then you get the water out and then you put the clothes back in and you put fresh water in to rinse them.

364
00:38:51.857 --> 00:38:54.857
That was a step aboard a washboard.

365
00:38:54.857 --> 00:38:57.000
<v CARHEEL>It was fun, though.

366
00:38:57.000 --> 00:39:06.402
<v BRAXTON>But anyway, we had one of those machines, and it would shake a lot and there were some shelves over it.

367
00:39:06.402 --> 00:39:14.460
And they had a glass, I’ll never forget, it was a green, about the color of that can,

368
00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:22.967
green, dark green flower pot that was sitting on the shelf and it fell and it hit my foot.

369
00:39:22.967 --> 00:39:24.310
I was barefooted.

370
00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:26.100
It cut my toe.

371
00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:31.921
I still have a scar across the first joint of my big toe.

372
00:39:31.921 --> 00:39:33.264
It cut me.

373
00:39:33.264 --> 00:39:35.950
I remember blood was [skeeting?] up.

374
00:39:35.950 --> 00:39:41.322
I assume I probably hollered a little, responded some kind of way.

375
00:39:41.322 --> 00:39:46.695
But they came back there to see what was wrong with me.

376
00:39:46.695 --> 00:39:54.753
They sit me down on the porch and they reach up with a stick and grabbed spider webs.

377
00:39:54.753 --> 00:39:57.887
It was spider webs everywhere back then.

378
00:39:57.887 --> 00:40:09.975
They wrapped the spider webs around the cut and then they put turpentine on it to keep it from getting infected and everything and it healed up.

379
00:40:09.975 --> 00:40:15.347
But we used to take turpentine orally as well for different things.

380
00:40:15.347 --> 00:40:27.435
You put a little turpentine in a teaspoon on a little sugar . . . maybe put a squeeze of lemon on it . . . all about disguising the taste of it.

381
00:40:27.435 --> 00:40:31.912
And you would do the same thing with castor oil.

382
00:40:31.912 --> 00:40:39.971
There were other . . . herbs and stuff that was . . . lots of them, that was used to treat various things.

383
00:40:39.971 --> 00:40:40.418
<v CARHEEL>Sassafras.

384
00:40:40.418 --> 00:40:40.866
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

385
00:40:40.866 --> 00:40:41.314
<v CARHEEL>Sassafras.

386
00:40:41.314 --> 00:40:41.762
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

387
00:40:41.762 --> 00:40:43.105
Well, sassafras is . . .

388
00:40:43.105 --> 00:40:44.000
<v CARHEEL>Polypore

389
00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:44.768
<v BRAXTON>Polypore tea.

390
00:40:44.768 --> 00:40:48.222
People would take polypore tea to clean them out.

391
00:40:48.222 --> 00:40:53.212
Polypore is made from . . . it’s actually a fungus that grows on oak trees,

392
00:40:53.212 --> 00:40:58.970
actually, and would boil it the same way to extract the medicinal stuff from it.

393
00:40:58.970 --> 00:40:59.737
Sassafras, huh?

394
00:40:59.737 --> 00:41:02.040
<v DRAGOON>Sorry, it’s called . . . What is it?

395
00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:02.424
<v BRAXTON>Polypore

396
00:41:02.424 --> 00:41:02.808
<v DRAGOON>Polypore?

397
00:41:02.808 --> 00:41:03.192
Polypore?

398
00:41:03.192 --> 00:41:10.485
<v BRAXTON>My cousin was just, about a month or so ago, saying, “Man, I need to buy myself some polypore.”

399
00:41:10.485 --> 00:41:14.323
And he was talking about where to get it from.

400
00:41:14.323 --> 00:41:17.394
<v CARHEEL>LCB is full of it in the front.

401
00:41:17.394 --> 00:41:19.697
<v BRAXTON>Oh yeah, a lot of places.

402
00:41:19.697 --> 00:41:20.848
<v DRAGOON>What is that?

403
00:41:20.848 --> 00:41:22.000
<v CARHEEL>LCB School.

404
00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:22.640
<v BRAXTON>I’m surprised.

405
00:41:22.640 --> 00:41:26.160
If I look, there’s probably some on your trees out there.

406
00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:28.720
<v CARHEEL>No, I don’t have any on my trees.

407
00:41:28.720 --> 00:41:30.000
<v BRAXTON>There’s not on it?

408
00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:31.920
<v DRAGOON>How do you make the medicine?

409
00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:32.880
How do you . . .

410
00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:34.800
<v BRAXTON>You just boil it and extract . . .

411
00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:35.120
<v DRAGOON>Oh.

412
00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:35.760
The properties?

413
00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:37.360
<v BRAXTON>. . . medicinal properties into the water.

414
00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:38.640
Then you drink it.

415
00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:39.280
You know?

416
00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:41.200
<v CARHEEL>I don't remember them drinking polypore.

417
00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:42.480
I remember them bathing . . .

418
00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:44.080
<v BRAXTON>Or you bathe in it.

419
00:41:44.080 --> 00:41:46.000
<v CARHEEL>. . . sores and stuff with it.

420
00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:55.487
<v BRAXTON>But a lot of the stuff that you use internally, you use externally in some form or fashion as well.

421
00:41:55.487 --> 00:41:58.808
And sassafras, they would make sassafras tea.

422
00:41:58.808 --> 00:42:02.603
But sassafras is the forerunner of root beer.

423
00:42:02.603 --> 00:42:08.295
That’s what root beer was originally made from was sassafras—root beer sodas.

424
00:42:08.295 --> 00:42:11.141
<v DRAGOON>So it tastes like root beer?

425
00:42:11.141 --> 00:42:11.615
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

426
00:42:11.615 --> 00:42:16.833
<v DRAGOON>So the sassafras tea tastes like root beer, a little bit?

427
00:42:16.833 --> 00:42:17.308
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

428
00:42:17.308 --> 00:42:22.051
But they eventually came to the point of carbonating it . . .

429
00:42:22.051 --> 00:42:23.000
<v DRAGOON>Right.

430
00:42:23.000 --> 00:42:25.028
<v BRAXTON>. . . then you had root beer soda.

431
00:42:25.028 --> 00:42:27.056
I mean, that’s what it was.

432
00:42:27.056 --> 00:42:30.099
<v DRAGOON>And sassafras, was that good for like colds, or . . .

433
00:42:30.099 --> 00:42:32.127
<v BRAXTON>It was good for some things.

434
00:42:32.127 --> 00:42:35.169
But it was a beverage, for the most part.

435
00:42:35.169 --> 00:42:35.845
<v DRAGOON>Oh, okay.

436
00:42:35.845 --> 00:42:40.239
<v BRAXTON>But it probably originated with the Indians and such back in the day.

437
00:42:40.239 --> 00:42:43.620
<v CARHEEL>I think it was good for stomachaches and stuff, too.

438
00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:47.000
<v DRAGOON>Were there any healers or treaters in that area?

439
00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:51.199
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah. [Laughs] There were healers in the area.

440
00:42:51.199 --> 00:43:00.062
When I was a kid, my mother didn’t cook beans because they, dry beans, because they made her sick.

441
00:43:00.062 --> 00:43:06.127
So my opportunity to eat beans were at school . . . because I loved beans.

442
00:43:06.127 --> 00:43:13.592
I had a friend named Toot and Toot lived three or four houses down the street.

443
00:43:13.592 --> 00:43:16.391
He was raised by his aunt.

444
00:43:16.391 --> 00:43:23.855
And a certain day of the week, his aunt, I think on Wednesdays, would cook beans.

445
00:43:23.855 --> 00:43:35.518
I would make an excuse to go play with Toot on that day so that I could partake in them beans that she cooked . . . boy,

446
00:43:35.518 --> 00:43:38.317
because they would really be good.

447
00:43:38.317 --> 00:43:47.647
So I was down there one day and we ate and I was still hanging out around there and everything.

448
00:43:47.647 --> 00:43:53.712
His aunt looked at him and she said, “You know, Toot,” or Elmo,

449
00:43:53.712 --> 00:44:00.243
I think she called him, “We need to take you to see Mr. Joe.”

450
00:44:00.243 --> 00:44:05.375
He has some kind of wart or something on his face.

451
00:44:05.375 --> 00:44:11.906
Mr. Joe was sort of a faith healer and he was an older gentleman.

452
00:44:11.906 --> 00:44:22.636
The most association I’d really had with him up to that point was we used to see him ride, he rode a bicycle.

453
00:44:22.636 --> 00:44:25.435
He was a pretty big fellow.

454
00:44:25.435 --> 00:44:31.500
He rode a bicycle with a big hat and he smoked a pipe.

455
00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:36.632
He had a big basket on the front of that bicycle.

456
00:44:36.632 --> 00:44:38.964
He was a cordial fellow.

457
00:44:38.964 --> 00:44:42.696
He lived a couple of miles from us.

458
00:44:42.696 --> 00:44:51.560
So I went down there with them to Mr. Joe house, and I don't know what I was expecting.

459
00:44:51.560 --> 00:45:03.690
But anyway, we got inside of Mr. Joe’s house and Mr. Joe had Toot sit in a chair, and then Mr. Joe sit in a chair.

460
00:45:03.690 --> 00:45:06.955
They was kind of facing each other.

461
00:45:06.955 --> 00:45:13.487
Toot was a kid, of course, so he was kind of in between Mr.

462
00:45:13.487 --> 00:45:14.420
<c.p>Toot’s legs.

463
00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:17.219
The chairs were real close together.

464
00:45:17.219 --> 00:45:27.016
And Mr. Toot was . . . Mr. Joe, I’m sorry, Mr. Joe was chewing on tobacco and he engaged Toot in some conversation.

465
00:45:27.016 --> 00:45:32.614
I guess he sensed that he had Toot’s full attention in conversation.

466
00:45:32.614 --> 00:45:35.413
He wasn’t preoccupied with other thoughts.

467
00:45:35.413 --> 00:45:43.344
And just out of nowhere, he spit that big wad of tobacco out and upside Toot’s face.

468
00:45:43.344 --> 00:45:53.607
About the same time that it hit his face, he put his hand on it and he rubbed it in like that.

469
00:45:53.607 --> 00:46:02.938
It happened so quick that Toot never had an opportunity to even react. [Laughs] It was over with and done.

470
00:46:02.938 --> 00:46:05.737
He just pushed his chair back.

471
00:46:05.737 --> 00:46:09.469
He said, “You’ll be all right now.” [laughs]

472
00:46:09.469 --> 00:46:10.868
<v DRAGOON>That was it.

473
00:46:10.868 --> 00:46:12.734
<v BRAXTON>That was it. [laughs]

474
00:46:12.734 --> 00:46:16.000
<v CARHEEL>That’s one of his favorite stories.

475
00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:17.834
<v BRAXTON>I was speechless, okay?

476
00:46:17.834 --> 00:46:21.502
I can’t tell you what happened after that.

477
00:46:21.502 --> 00:46:26.546
I don't know if it got worse or it got better.

478
00:46:26.546 --> 00:46:29.755
But I mean, he got the treatment.

479
00:46:29.755 --> 00:46:31.590
That was the treatment.

480
00:46:31.590 --> 00:46:35.716
He went out there and his aunt was satisfied.

481
00:46:35.716 --> 00:46:37.092
That was it.

482
00:46:37.092 --> 00:46:46.262
That was my only personal experience with being at a faith healer, but it was a number of them around.

483
00:46:46.262 --> 00:46:49.472
<v CARHEEL>Yeah, I have a story about that.

484
00:46:49.472 --> 00:46:54.974
My second . . . no, my baby brother was born with a big ball,

485
00:46:54.974 --> 00:47:00.934
big white ball, under his tongue and he was supposed to have surgery.

486
00:47:00.934 --> 00:47:05.520
They had to go to New Orleans for the surgery.

487
00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:10.105
The day before they left, which was a Sunday afternoon,

488
00:47:10.105 --> 00:47:17.900
my mama took him to one of the faith healers and all he did, though, was pray.

489
00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:19.734
He didn’t use the

490
00:47:19.734 --> 00:47:22.485
<c.p>other stuff. [Laughs] He just prayed.

491
00:47:22.485 --> 00:47:31.655
She took him to this man’s house and the man prayed for him that afternoon and they came back home.

492
00:47:31.655 --> 00:47:41.742
And the next morning, when he woke up, the day he was supposed to go for the surgery, the ball was gone.

493
00:47:41.742 --> 00:47:42.201
Disappeared.

494
00:47:42.201 --> 00:47:44.952
<v BRAXTON>I vaguely remember something about that.

495
00:47:44.952 --> 00:47:54.122
<v CARHEEL>And it had been there for . . . he was born with it, and it had been there for a good while.

496
00:47:54.122 --> 00:47:56.873
It just disappeared and was gone.

497
00:47:56.873 --> 00:48:01.000
So he didn’t have to have the surgery.

498
00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:01.418
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

499
00:48:01.418 --> 00:48:06.013
Do you remember who that was that your family went to?

500
00:48:06.013 --> 00:48:08.101
Was it a family friend?

501
00:48:08.101 --> 00:48:11.443
<v CARHEEL>Who was that man’s name back there by . . .

502
00:48:11.443 --> 00:48:12.278
<v BRAXTON>By where?

503
00:48:12.278 --> 00:48:14.367
<v CARHEEL>Was that Ms. Rita’s husband?

504
00:48:14.367 --> 00:48:15.203
<v BRAXTON>Where at?

505
00:48:15.203 --> 00:48:23.557
<v CARHEEL>Back there by where Phyllis and them used to live . . . from his ma . . . back there by [Dres Marie?] and them.

506
00:48:23.557 --> 00:48:25.646
Was that Ms. Rita’s husband?

507
00:48:25.646 --> 00:48:27.316
What was his name?

508
00:48:27.316 --> 00:48:30.241
<v BRAXTON>You’re not talking about Reverend Martin Edwards.

509
00:48:30.241 --> 00:48:31.911
<v CARHEEL>No, not Martin Edwards.

510
00:48:31.911 --> 00:48:34.000
<v BRAXTON>Oh, I don’t recall.

511
00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:34.482
<v CARHEEL>Yeah.

512
00:48:34.482 --> 00:48:38.824
That boy who used to drive the orange Volkswagen.

513
00:48:38.824 --> 00:48:39.306
<v BRAXTON>[LeDoux?]

514
00:48:39.306 --> 00:48:39.788
<v CARHEEL>Yes.

515
00:48:39.788 --> 00:48:41.718
That’s what it was.

516
00:48:41.718 --> 00:48:42.200
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

517
00:48:42.200 --> 00:48:44.612
<v CARHEEL>What was his first name?

518
00:48:44.612 --> 00:48:46.541
<v BRAXTON>Ed [LeDoux?], I believe.

519
00:48:46.541 --> 00:48:47.988
Yeah, Ed [LeDoux?]

520
00:48:47.988 --> 00:48:50.882
<v CARHEEL>I think that’s who it was.

521
00:48:50.882 --> 00:48:55.224
<v BRAXTON>I said Reverend Martin, but it’s not Reverend Martin.

522
00:48:55.224 --> 00:48:57.635
It was Reverend Ed LeDoux.

523
00:48:57.635 --> 00:49:02.941
<v CARHEEL>Yeah, it was [LeDoux?], I think, that she took him to.

524
00:49:02.941 --> 00:49:03.424
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

525
00:49:03.424 --> 00:49:03.906
<v CARHEEL>Yep.

526
00:49:03.906 --> 00:49:05.835
And it was gone.

527
00:49:05.835 --> 00:49:07.765
<v BRAXTON>I vaguely remember that.

528
00:49:07.765 --> 00:49:09.694
Some aspects of it.

529
00:49:09.694 --> 00:49:11.624
<v CARHEEL>[low audio] Mm hmm.

530
00:49:11.624 --> 00:49:13.553
I remember that well.

531
00:49:13.553 --> 00:49:15.000
We were [?]

532
00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:17.692
<v BRAXTON>There were a lot of others.

533
00:49:17.692 --> 00:49:23.974
There were fortune tellers during the period of time when I was coming up.

534
00:49:23.974 --> 00:49:28.013
One used to advertise on the radio quite often.

535
00:49:28.013 --> 00:49:32.949
Didn’t live in, this one in particular didn’t live in Mossville.

536
00:49:32.949 --> 00:49:35.641
But it lived in Lake Charles.

537
00:49:35.641 --> 00:49:36.987
But Mother Divine . . .

538
00:49:36.987 --> 00:49:38.782
<v CARHEEL>I do remember her.

539
00:49:38.782 --> 00:49:42.821
<v BRAXTON>She was popular for many, many, many, many years.

540
00:49:42.821 --> 00:49:43.269
Around . . .

541
00:49:43.269 --> 00:49:45.064
<v DRAGOON>Even folks in Mossville . . .

542
00:49:45.064 --> 00:49:45.513
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

543
00:49:45.513 --> 00:49:50.000
<v DRAGOON>Did folks in Mossville go to see her, too?

544
00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:51.789
<v BRAXTON>Oh, everybody did.

545
00:49:51.789 --> 00:49:58.947
I mean, Lake Charles and Mossville were both much smaller communities then.

546
00:49:58.947 --> 00:50:09.088
This is just unbelievable how the population has changed from what it was back then, you know.

547
00:50:09.088 --> 00:50:10.281
But yeah.

548
00:50:10.281 --> 00:50:24.000
<v CARHEEL>Back then we had a bus and you could just hop on the bus and go see Mother Divine and anybody else.

549
00:50:24.000 --> 00:50:34.500
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, the city, the Lake Charles city bus, used to operate in some unincorporated areas as well, like Mossville.

550
00:50:34.500 --> 00:50:41.684
So that was one of the ways that—the other thing that was really,

551
00:50:41.684 --> 00:50:49.974
really popular back then, for instance, we didn’t have a car for periods of times.

552
00:50:49.974 --> 00:51:10.974
The way we got to different places and did different things is you had certain people you could depend on to call them and they not even related to you . . . maybe not even a close friend or anything,

553
00:51:10.974 --> 00:51:25.342
but you could depend on them to like take you to pay your electric bill, or make groceries and you would pay them a few dollars.

554
00:51:25.342 --> 00:51:28.658
That was a very popular thing.

555
00:51:28.658 --> 00:51:32.526
It was typically people that didn’t work.

556
00:51:32.526 --> 00:51:34.737
And gas was cheap.

557
00:51:34.737 --> 00:51:43.026
If you paid them two or three dollars, they had made some money that day.

558
00:51:43.026 --> 00:51:50.763
So it was a number . . . I can think right off Mr. Toby, Mr. Toby . . .

559
00:51:50.763 --> 00:51:51.316
<v CARHEEL>Barti

560
00:51:51.316 --> 00:51:51.868
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

561
00:51:51.868 --> 00:51:52.421
<v CARHEEL>Barti

562
00:51:52.421 --> 00:51:54.079
<v BRAXTON>Mr. Toby Barti.

563
00:51:54.079 --> 00:51:55.737
Mr. Nelson Delafosse.

564
00:51:55.737 --> 00:51:56.842
Mr. [Footer?].

565
00:51:56.842 --> 00:51:57.947
<v CARHEEL>Ms. Williams?

566
00:51:57.947 --> 00:51:58.500
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

567
00:51:58.500 --> 00:51:59.605
<v CARHEEL>Ms. Williams.

568
00:51:59.605 --> 00:52:00.711
<v BRAXTON>Ms. Williams.

569
00:52:00.711 --> 00:52:04.579
I was trying to think of her . . .

570
00:52:04.579 --> 00:52:05.684
<v CARHEEL>Mr. Bates.

571
00:52:05.684 --> 00:52:06.789
<v BRAXTON>Mr. Bates.

572
00:52:06.789 --> 00:52:09.000
<v CARHEEL>Mr. Albert Bates.

573
00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:14.351
<v DRAGOON>Ms. Williams doesn’t happen to be Ms. Betty Williams?

574
00:52:14.351 --> 00:52:14.946
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

575
00:52:14.946 --> 00:52:15.541
<v CARHEEL>No.

576
00:52:15.541 --> 00:52:17.919
<v DRAGOON>Not Ms. Betty Williams.

577
00:52:17.919 --> 00:52:23.270
<v CARHEEL>Not Betty Williams, no. What was her first name?

578
00:52:23.270 --> 00:52:28.027
<v BRAXTON>And used to stay over there by [Delaville?]

579
00:52:28.027 --> 00:52:31.000
<v CARHEEL>That was Albert Bates.

580
00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:33.899
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, that was Mr. Bates, yeah.

581
00:52:33.899 --> 00:52:43.079
It was a number of people that you could just depend on to take you to do those things.

582
00:52:43.079 --> 00:52:45.978
That was how you moved around.

583
00:52:45.978 --> 00:52:50.809
And they would just sit there and wait for you.

584
00:52:50.809 --> 00:52:58.539
It may be things you were doing that took you two or three hours, or whatever.

585
00:52:58.539 --> 00:52:59.506
<v CARHEEL>Yeah, laundry.

586
00:52:59.506 --> 00:52:59.989
<v BRAXTON>Laundry.

587
00:52:59.989 --> 00:53:04.337
You know, when it got to be laundromats around.

588
00:53:04.337 --> 00:53:12.551
But when I was a kid, I clearly remember washing clothes by hand with a rub board.

589
00:53:12.551 --> 00:53:14.000
<v CARHEEL>Oh, yeah.

590
00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:17.600
<v BRAXTON>But what I preferred over the rub board was to stomp.

591
00:53:17.600 --> 00:53:20.873
We would put those clothes in that number three tub.

592
00:53:20.873 --> 00:53:30.036
You’d have some short pants on and you’d get in there and you’d stomp them clothes and they’d come clean just like they was in a washing machine.

593
00:53:30.036 --> 00:53:32.655
And you just got outside and you stomped.

594
00:53:32.655 --> 00:53:36.582
They wrung them out and then you put some fresh water in.

595
00:53:36.582 --> 00:53:38.545
You stomped the rest of them.

596
00:53:38.545 --> 00:53:40.509
<v CARHEEL>Stomped the rest of them. [Laughs]

597
00:53:40.509 --> 00:53:43.782
<v BRAXTON>And then you hang them on the line to dry.

598
00:53:43.782 --> 00:53:45.418
That’s the way it was.

599
00:53:45.418 --> 00:53:47.055
<v CARHEEL>We’ve done all of that.

600
00:53:47.055 --> 00:53:47.382
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

601
00:53:47.382 --> 00:53:50.000
<v CARHEEL>I said, we’ve done all of that.

602
00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:50.456
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

603
00:53:50.456 --> 00:53:55.929
And even though we had . . . like I was saying, mentioned earlier about,

604
00:53:55.929 --> 00:54:02.769
we had a deep well before my father died because prior to the deep well,

605
00:54:02.769 --> 00:54:09.610
we had a shallow well, which was what they call a six- inch tower well.

606
00:54:09.610 --> 00:54:15.995
There was a pipe that big around which is still there, but not usable.

607
00:54:15.995 --> 00:54:21.467
It went down only like twenty-some odd feet down in the ground,

608
00:54:21.467 --> 00:54:29.676
as opposed to a deep well that’s common today where it’s like 500 feet down in the ground.

609
00:54:29.676 --> 00:54:36.516
After Hurricane Audrey came through in June 1957, we started having problems with that well,

610
00:54:36.516 --> 00:54:42.901
thinking it might have been related to Hurricane Audrey because it started getting salty.

611
00:54:42.901 --> 00:54:49.742
Hurricane Audrey drove a lot of salt water, not all the way to our house,

612
00:54:49.742 --> 00:54:58.407
but it goes through the salt system and eventually ends up into the shallower waters before it’s filtered out.

613
00:54:58.407 --> 00:55:02.055
So we had to get a deep well.

614
00:55:02.055 --> 00:55:13.456
So we had the deep well, which I remember it was great water to drink out of that electric deep well, that electric-operated deep well.

615
00:55:13.456 --> 00:55:23.945
But the shallow well, when we had it, we used to have to manually get the water out of it with a bucket.

616
00:55:23.945 --> 00:55:32.154
We had like an arch that went over the top of the pipe up maybe eight foot high.

617
00:55:32.154 --> 00:55:32.610
It

618
00:55:32.610 --> 00:55:43.555
<c.p>had a rope and a pulley on it and you had a bucket that was about thirty-six inches long and about that big around.

619
00:55:43.555 --> 00:55:51.308
It was just big enough . . . small enough, rather, to go through that hole down to the well.

620
00:55:51.308 --> 00:55:57.692
You dropped it down in and it had a one-way valve on the bottom.

621
00:55:57.692 --> 00:56:06.813
It allowed water to come in it, but it wouldn’t allow it to come out when you pulled it up.

622
00:56:06.813 --> 00:56:12.742
You pulled it up and after you pulled it up out of there,

623
00:56:12.742 --> 00:56:19.126
you had to dump it into another bucket and you just keep repeating that.

624
00:56:19.126 --> 00:56:25.967
That’s what you had to do if you was washing clothes or cooking or whatever.

625
00:56:25.967 --> 00:56:29.615
That’s how you had to get the water.

626
00:56:29.615 --> 00:56:36.000
So that was one of the things that we had to deal with.

627
00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:41.817
<v DRAGOON>How did your family split up chores growing up?

628
00:56:41.817 --> 00:56:53.451
<v BRAXTON>[Laughs] It wasn’t . . . Girls typically did inside work: dusting and helping the cook, wash dishes, and so forth.

629
00:56:53.451 --> 00:56:55.390
And boys would . . .

630
00:56:55.390 --> 00:56:57.329
<v CARHEEL>Cleaning the china.

631
00:56:57.329 --> 00:56:57.976
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

632
00:56:57.976 --> 00:57:01.207
<v CARHEEL>I said cleaning the china.

633
00:57:01.207 --> 00:57:03.146
<v BRAXTON>Cleaning the what?

634
00:57:03.146 --> 00:57:03.793
<v CARHEEL>China.

635
00:57:03.793 --> 00:57:05.732
And the silverware.

636
00:57:05.732 --> 00:57:06.378
<v BRAXTON>Oh.

637
00:57:06.378 --> 00:57:14.134
She said that because she used to hate that, seriously hate that.

638
00:57:14.134 --> 00:57:19.951
She would talk about that all the time. [Laughs]

639
00:57:19.951 --> 00:57:20.598
<v CARHEEL>Silverware.

640
00:57:20.598 --> 00:57:27.061
Standing there, polishing, for days because it had to shine.

641
00:57:27.061 --> 00:57:29.000
Come on!

642
00:57:29.000 --> 00:57:33.982
<v BRAXTON>Her mother, we used to have the blinds like you have now,

643
00:57:33.982 --> 00:57:40.626
the blinds you have the little thing you can turn and it open and close them.

644
00:57:40.626 --> 00:57:43.947
Well back then, they were all pretty standard.

645
00:57:43.947 --> 00:57:47.684
They didn’t make a lot of varieties of things.

646
00:57:47.684 --> 00:57:53.082
So the blinds back then typically was all about two- inch wide blinds.

647
00:57:53.082 --> 00:57:55.573
And her mother, I don't know,

648
00:57:55.573 --> 00:58:05.123
she hated . . . I wouldn’t even remember this if it wasn’t for the fact that she was so emotional when she talked about having,

649
00:58:05.123 --> 00:58:09.690
Mama would make her clean those blinds about twice a year.

650
00:58:09.690 --> 00:58:11.766
<v CARHEEL>That was serious work, though.

651
00:58:11.766 --> 00:58:14.673
<v BRAXTON>Well, it was, to clean them blinds.

652
00:58:14.673 --> 00:58:17.164
You had to wipe them down.

653
00:58:17.164 --> 00:58:19.655
<v CARHEEL>And it wasn’t wiping them down.

654
00:58:19.655 --> 00:58:22.146
You had to actually clean them.

655
00:58:22.146 --> 00:58:25.053
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, well, you had to clean them.

656
00:58:25.053 --> 00:58:26.713
That was cleaning them.

657
00:58:26.713 --> 00:58:29.205
<v CARHEEL>It wasn’t the little vinyl things.

658
00:58:29.205 --> 00:58:33.357
That was the wood, and you had to scrub that.

659
00:58:33.357 --> 00:58:35.848
<v BRAXTON>Those weren’t wood there for us.

660
00:58:35.848 --> 00:58:36.678
They were . . .

661
00:58:36.678 --> 00:58:37.509
<v CARHEEL>The vinyl?

662
00:58:37.509 --> 00:58:38.754
<v BRAXTON>They were metal.

663
00:58:38.754 --> 00:58:40.000
<v CARHEEL>Yeah, metal.

664
00:58:40.000 --> 00:58:43.234
<v BRAXTON>But they still had to clean them.

665
00:58:43.234 --> 00:58:49.240
It wasn’t as simple as taking a duster and just dust across them.

666
00:58:49.240 --> 00:58:56.632
You had to use soap and water because back then, your houses didn’t have air conditioning.

667
00:58:56.632 --> 00:58:58.480
We wasn’t air conditioned.

668
00:58:58.480 --> 00:59:00.789
So your doors were open.

669
00:59:00.789 --> 00:59:05.409
A thousand kids going in and out all the time.

670
00:59:05.409 --> 00:59:10.953
So there was always dust on the floors to be swept up.

671
00:59:10.953 --> 00:59:15.111
So sweeping the floors was one thing . . . inside work.

672
00:59:15.111 --> 00:59:18.807
Outside, yards were what they called swept yards.

673
00:59:18.807 --> 00:59:22.965
A lot of people didn’t have grass at all.

674
00:59:22.965 --> 00:59:30.819
It just didn’t grow because one thing, there was so many kids that the grass couldn’t grow.

675
00:59:30.819 --> 00:59:42.368
I mean, it literally just . . . So you actually would just like sweep and maybe rake the yard for little things that would get over it,

676
00:59:42.368 --> 00:59:44.678
over a period of time.

677
00:59:44.678 --> 00:59:50.684
In fact, I was reading just yesterday about Lyndon Baine Johnson’s birth home.

678
00:59:50.684 --> 00:59:59.000
He said they had a swept yard, and that’s what we had, too, is a swept yard.

679
00:59:59.000 --> 01:00:03.064
You had animals roaming, if you had any.

680
01:00:03.064 --> 01:00:11.192
At one point in time, we used to have . . . we never had a lot of animals.

681
01:00:11.192 --> 01:00:16.272
We never had hogs, which was a very common thing.

682
01:00:16.272 --> 01:00:25.416
All around us, people behind us, my uncle on the side of us, I mean, everybody raised hogs.

683
01:00:25.416 --> 01:00:36.084
We never had any until I was a grown man and my stepfather had one hog that he raised one time,

684
01:00:36.084 --> 01:00:40.656
and it was just for the novelty of it.

685
01:00:40.656 --> 01:00:49.292
But it used to be . . . that was an important part of surviving was to have a hog.

686
01:00:49.292 --> 01:00:54.880
So boys tend to be involved with stuff like that now.

687
01:00:54.880 --> 01:01:05.548
I had a horse named Curly that my father had got when . . . it was there as long as I can remember,

688
01:01:05.548 --> 01:01:07.072
probably before I

689
01:01:07.072 --> 01:01:08.088
<c.p>was born.

690
01:01:08.088 --> 01:01:19.264
This horse was captured wild because, at the time, there was still some wild horses in the heavily wooded areas around Mossville.

691
01:01:19.264 --> 01:01:22.820
They had some large expanses of land.

692
01:01:22.820 --> 01:01:26.376
This horse was extremely mean . . . extremely mean.

693
01:01:26.376 --> 01:01:35.012
Over the years, several, a number of good cowboys have rode him to try to break him.

694
01:01:35.012 --> 01:01:38.568
And it only lasted for the moment.

695
01:01:38.568 --> 01:01:44.156
I actually had him for years and never, ever rode him.

696
01:01:44.156 --> 01:01:47.204
Used to feed him every day.

697
01:01:47.204 --> 01:01:50.252
In fact, he was very mean.

698
01:01:50.252 --> 01:01:56.348
We had chickens and we had dogs and we had a horse.

699
01:01:56.348 --> 01:02:00.920
We had some ducks at some period of time.

700
01:02:00.920 --> 01:02:06.000
Used to feed all them different animals, you know.

701
01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:12.853
The horse had a trough that was just right over the edge of his fence.

702
01:02:12.853 --> 01:02:18.335
So you could walk to the fence and push the feed over.

703
01:02:18.335 --> 01:02:26.558
One day I went out there to feed him, and of course he would have spillage and stuff,

704
01:02:26.558 --> 01:02:32.954
and the chickens and stuff would all run up there to try to eat.

705
01:02:32.954 --> 01:02:42.091
I’m standing there and the chickens were coming round, so the horse would kind of throw his head as in,

706
01:02:42.091 --> 01:02:48.030
all right, you all chickens, you all can get away from this trough.

707
01:02:48.030 --> 01:02:53.513
They’d step back a couple of feet and they come right back.

708
01:02:53.513 --> 01:03:00.822
And finally that horse just reached and caught a chicken and ate it . . . a live chicken.

709
01:03:00.822 --> 01:03:01.279
Okay?

710
01:03:01.279 --> 01:03:05.848
We had been wondering where all the chickens was going.

711
01:03:05.848 --> 01:03:13.157
We thought possums and all kind of stuff was getting them on the roofs at night.

712
01:03:13.157 --> 01:03:18.183
But this horse I think had developed a taste for chickens.

713
01:03:18.183 --> 01:03:23.208
There was another point of that story that happened years later.

714
01:03:23.208 --> 01:03:30.061
But I just couldn’t tell this about this horse unless I tell this piece, too.

715
01:03:30.061 --> 01:03:36.000
And that is that . . . that happened when I was a kid . . . teenager.

716
01:03:36.000 --> 01:03:47.079
But years later, in the later ‘80s, my youngest daughter and one of her mama’s brothers, Uncle Johnny, we, me, him and Jamar,

717
01:03:47.079 --> 01:03:55.750
went to visit one of my mother’s brothers that lived on the other side of Houston [Texas] . . . Frederick.

718
01:03:55.750 --> 01:04:01.049
We had been there for a couple of days and everything.

719
01:04:01.049 --> 01:04:03.939
My daughter and my uncle’s daughter,

720
01:04:03.939 --> 01:04:14.537
they had free run of about 400 acres of land that he was on and they went out and picked some pecans.

721
01:04:14.537 --> 01:04:17.427
These pecans were very tiny, too

722
01:04:17.427 --> 01:04:22.244
<c.p>small to really eat, but they were very good pecans.

723
01:04:22.244 --> 01:04:24.652
They were full of oil.

724
01:04:24.652 --> 01:04:28.506
They would actually buy pecans in the area.

725
01:04:28.506 --> 01:04:34.768
So they picked like a hundred-pound sack full of these little tiny pecans.

726
01:04:34.768 --> 01:04:42.476
And the next day, we went to the feed store, where they actually bought those pecans.

727
01:04:42.476 --> 01:04:47.293
I think they used the press oil out of them.

728
01:04:47.293 --> 01:04:49.220
Anyway, they sold them.

729
01:04:49.220 --> 01:04:55.000
And they got almost one hundred dollars or so for them.

730
01:04:55.000 --> 01:05:00.567
While that was all going on, my uncle that I went down there with,

731
01:05:00.567 --> 01:05:06.135
Uncle Johnny, he and I were standing on the dock at the feed store.

732
01:05:06.135 --> 01:05:19.257
My uncle from Texas was standing on the other end of the dock and he saw these three guys that were loading some feed onto a truck and he started talking to them,

733
01:05:19.257 --> 01:05:20.450
he knew them.

734
01:05:20.450 --> 01:05:26.018
He asked them, he said, “Hey, you all guys, how you all daddy doing?”

735
01:05:26.018 --> 01:05:28.404
They say, “Oh, Daddy’s doing fine.”

736
01:05:28.404 --> 01:05:31.982
My uncle said, “He still got all them horses?”

737
01:05:31.982 --> 01:05:35.164
They say, “Yeah, Dad’s still got the horses.”

738
01:05:35.164 --> 01:05:39.140
So my uncle said, “What about that old chicken-eating horse?

739
01:05:39.140 --> 01:05:41.129
Has he still got that?”

740
01:05:41.129 --> 01:05:43.117
“Yeah, he’s still got that.”

741
01:05:43.117 --> 01:05:53.456
And when he said that, my uncle that was standing with me, he kind of perked up and walked over there to listen to the conversation.

742
01:05:53.456 --> 01:05:54.649
I did, too.

743
01:05:54.649 --> 01:05:57.433
They really didn’t talk about that anymore.

744
01:05:57.433 --> 01:05:59.023
So we went on.

745
01:05:59.023 --> 01:06:03.000
That was like 8:30, nine o’clock in the morning.

746
01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:11.963
Well late that evening, right at dark, my uncle from here that we went there together, we were sitting on the porch.

747
01:06:11.963 --> 01:06:14.407
My other uncle was making coffee.

748
01:06:14.407 --> 01:06:25.000
So while it was just the two of us on the porch, he told me, he said, “George,” he said, “I’ve got to apologize to you.”

749
01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:26.222
I said, “Apologize?

750
01:06:26.222 --> 01:06:27.037
For what?”

751
01:06:27.037 --> 01:06:31.519
He said, and this was a man in his mid-seventies, okay?

752
01:06:31.519 --> 01:06:40.889
He said, “You remember a couple, three or four years ago you was at the house and you was getting ready to leave.

753
01:06:40.889 --> 01:06:46.593
And you told Rosa and I this story about this horse eating this chicken?”

754
01:06:46.593 --> 01:06:47.815
I said, “Yeah.”

755
01:06:47.815 --> 01:06:52.296
He said, “Well, when you left,” he said, “I told Rose.

756
01:06:52.296 --> 01:06:58.815
We looked at each other and we say, ‘Rose, you see how that boy sit there,

757
01:06:58.815 --> 01:07:03.296
told that damn lie about that horse eating that chicken? [Laughs]

758
01:07:03.296 --> 01:07:04.926
<c.p>With a straight face.’”

759
01:07:04.926 --> 01:07:12.259
He said, “Well, I would have died and went to my grave thinking you were lying about that.

760
01:07:12.259 --> 01:07:20.000
I ain’t never heard of no horse eating no chicken, until I heard my own brother talking about it.

761
01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:22.444
About the same thing this morning.”

762
01:07:22.444 --> 01:07:23.667
I said, “Oh.

763
01:07:23.667 --> 01:07:24.074
Okay.”

764
01:07:24.074 --> 01:07:28.556
So I’ve told that story to other guys I work with.

765
01:07:28.556 --> 01:07:32.222
They just give me the same kind of thing.

766
01:07:32.222 --> 01:07:37.111
You know, every once in a while I see one of them.

767
01:07:37.111 --> 01:07:38.333
Years have passed.

768
01:07:38.333 --> 01:07:42.000
They say, “How about that chicken-eating horse?” [Laughs]

769
01:07:42.000 --> 01:07:45.879
<v DRAGOON>Did you have that horse for a long time?

770
01:07:45.879 --> 01:07:49.759
<v BRAXTON>That horse went out the same way he lived.

771
01:07:49.759 --> 01:07:51.914
He was mean and hateful.

772
01:07:51.914 --> 01:07:54.931
We had a very, very bad thunderstorm,

773
01:07:54.931 --> 01:08:04.845
lightning storm came through one night and we didn’t have a barn or anything for him and lightning hit him and killed him.

774
01:08:04.845 --> 01:08:13.897
The next day we called a company that was, I guess it was pretty common knowledge thing then, who you call.

775
01:08:13.897 --> 01:08:16.052
And we called this company.

776
01:08:16.052 --> 01:08:18.638
They came out of Beaumont, Texas.

777
01:08:18.638 --> 01:08:28.552
They came all the way to Mossville and they ran some cables like 200 feet back into the backyard and hooked onto him,

778
01:08:28.552 --> 01:08:34.586
and drug him to the front, and they hauled him off to make soap.

779
01:08:34.586 --> 01:08:37.172
They made soap out of him.

780
01:08:37.172 --> 01:08:37.603
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

781
01:08:37.603 --> 01:08:41.052
<v BRAXTON>And that was the end of Curly. [Laughs]

782
01:08:41.052 --> 01:08:41.483
<v DRAGOON>Wow.

783
01:08:41.483 --> 01:08:44.500
So you took care of Curly, mostly?

784
01:08:44.500 --> 01:08:47.517
<v BRAXTON>It was no care except feeding him.

785
01:08:47.517 --> 01:08:47.948
Yeah.

786
01:08:47.948 --> 01:08:49.241
Yeah, I did.

787
01:08:49.241 --> 01:08:49.672
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

788
01:08:49.672 --> 01:08:52.690
So he was kind of your horse.

789
01:08:52.690 --> 01:08:54.845
<v BRAXTON>He was my horse, yeah.

790
01:08:54.845 --> 01:08:57.000
<v DRAGOON>He was your horse.

791
01:08:57.000 --> 01:08:57.538
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

792
01:08:57.538 --> 01:09:02.923
That’s the way I always considered, it was my horse.

793
01:09:02.923 --> 01:09:04.538
Nobody rode him.

794
01:09:04.538 --> 01:09:09.923
He was just . . . [Laughs] You couldn't do nothing with him.

795
01:09:09.923 --> 01:09:14.769
You couldn’t even go in the pen with him.

796
01:09:14.769 --> 01:09:19.615
If you went in the pen, through the pen,

797
01:09:19.615 --> 01:09:30.923
you ran through the pen to make sure you got across before he got too close because he was just mean.

798
01:09:30.923 --> 01:09:34.154
<v DRAGOON>That sounds kind of scary. [Laughs]

799
01:09:34.154 --> 01:09:36.308
<v BRAXTON>Just pure mean, yeah.

800
01:09:36.308 --> 01:09:37.385
<v DRAGOON>Let’s see.

801
01:09:37.385 --> 01:09:48.154
How were decisions made in your house about things like school and moving and jobs and even approval of marriage?

802
01:09:48.154 --> 01:09:49.231
<v BRAXTON>Of what?

803
01:09:49.231 --> 01:09:50.846
<v DRAGOON>Approval of marriage.

804
01:09:50.846 --> 01:09:53.000
People getting married?

805
01:09:53.000 --> 01:10:00.842
<v BRAXTON>I don’t really know how to . . . certain things, you just . . . was clear to you.

806
01:10:00.842 --> 01:10:12.044
You knew you had to go to school and you went; there was no question in your mind around that.

807
01:10:12.044 --> 01:10:19.326
Whatever activities we were involved in in school were supported by my mother,

808
01:10:19.326 --> 01:10:26.047
which was . . . back then it really wasn’t a whole lot of things.

809
01:10:26.047 --> 01:10:30.528
I personally never participated in formal sports activities.

810
01:10:30.528 --> 01:10:35.570
But I was in the band for a while.

811
01:10:35.570 --> 01:10:41.731
I was in a couple of plays, stage plays, at school.

812
01:10:41.731 --> 01:10:44.532
Yeah, a couple of plays.

813
01:10:44.532 --> 01:10:50.693
I remember . . . you probably heard of the dance, the mashed potatoes?

814
01:10:50.693 --> 01:10:54.614
Well, that’s way back in the ‘50s.

815
01:10:54.614 --> 01:11:04.136
I was part of a stage play that involved some dancing and I didn’t like to dance.

816
01:11:04.136 --> 01:11:11.418
I don't know how I ended up in the play to begin with,

817
01:11:11.418 --> 01:11:22.060
because I don't think it was really my choice to say, “Oh, I want to be in this play.”

818
01:11:22.060 --> 01:11:25.981
I don't think it went like that.

819
01:11:25.981 --> 01:11:33.263
I’ll never forget that the floor was a wood varnish floor, the stage.

820
01:11:33.263 --> 01:11:40.544
They made us pull our shoes off to keep from marring the stage.

821
01:11:40.544 --> 01:11:45.025
And I had a hole in my sock.

822
01:11:45.025 --> 01:11:48.386
That just done it for me.

823
01:11:48.386 --> 01:12:00.149
I mean, I couldn’t focus on nothing at all, because all I could think about was this hole in my sock.

824
01:12:00.149 --> 01:12:07.991
I had . . . that was the last play or anything I ever wanted to be,

825
01:12:07.991 --> 01:12:13.592
show you how something could be such a powerful influence.

826
01:12:13.592 --> 01:12:17.513
But that hole in my sock, that . . .

827
01:12:17.513 --> 01:12:18.633
<v CARHEEL>[low audio] [?]

828
01:12:18.633 --> 01:12:24.234
<v BRAXTON>I can’t even explain to you how powerful that was.

829
01:12:24.234 --> 01:12:31.516
I mean, it was terrible! [Laughs] I still don’t like to dance today.

830
01:12:31.516 --> 01:12:37.677
It all go back to that hole in my sock. [Laughs]

831
01:12:37.677 --> 01:12:47.199
<v DRAGOON>Did your mother influence your decision to be in the band and to be in the play?

832
01:12:47.199 --> 01:12:50.000
Did she convince you?

833
01:12:50.000 --> 01:12:56.121
<v BRAXTON>I played the drums for a very short period of time.

834
01:12:56.121 --> 01:13:10.032
But my interests, I was always so interested in so many different things that I couldn’t nail myself down to do some one certain thing.

835
01:13:10.032 --> 01:13:17.266
So when I was like fourteen, I started working; I had a job.

836
01:13:17.266 --> 01:13:23.387
And to this day, until I retired, I was never unemployed.

837
01:13:23.387 --> 01:13:32.290
I can account for one or two months of unemployment since I was fourteen years old.

838
01:13:32.290 --> 01:13:39.524
I worked at local restaurants and hotels, like the Pelican Hotel in Sulphur,

839
01:13:39.524 --> 01:13:47.315
and Sheraton Chateau Charles, and the Lake View Motel and Restaurant . . . places like that.

840
01:13:47.315 --> 01:13:47.871
I . . .

841
01:13:47.871 --> 01:13:52.323
<v CARHEEL>Didn’t you start off building pallets or something?

842
01:13:52.323 --> 01:13:54.548
<v BRAXTON>I never did pallets.

843
01:13:54.548 --> 01:13:59.000
<v CARHEEL>Or working up there, at that sawmill?

844
01:13:59.000 --> 01:14:13.066
<v BRAXTON>But I had a number of odd jobs when I was a kid, such as the uncle that I told you that we went and visited,

845
01:14:13.066 --> 01:14:24.967
when he moved from Mossville to Texas, he was living right next door to us, basically, and he had a huge shop.

846
01:14:24.967 --> 01:14:28.754
And he tore this big shop down.

847
01:14:28.754 --> 01:14:31.459
It was a wood workshop.

848
01:14:31.459 --> 01:14:42.279
All that lumber and stuff, he had planned to take it and rebuild it as a barn or such over,

849
01:14:42.279 --> 01:14:52.016
so he gave me the job of pulling the nails out of the boards and straightening them out.

850
01:14:52.016 --> 01:14:54.721
And I had that job.

851
01:14:54.721 --> 01:14:58.508
I cleaned bricks for a little while.

852
01:14:58.508 --> 01:15:08.787
I used to clean bricks, two bricks for a penny . . . different little odd jobs things around that I did.

853
01:15:08.787 --> 01:15:14.197
I used to like to spend time fishing and hunting.

854
01:15:14.197 --> 01:15:22.311
Fishing was one of the things that was a family event . . . frequent family event for

855
01:15:22.311 --> 01:15:23.934
<c.p>the whole family.

856
01:15:23.934 --> 01:15:33.672
The whole family not just went, but looked forward to it, each and every one, to going fishing.

857
01:15:33.672 --> 01:15:36.918
<v DRAGOON>How many siblings did you have?

858
01:15:36.918 --> 01:15:38.000
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

859
01:15:38.000 --> 01:15:40.737
<v DRAGOON>How many siblings did you have?

860
01:15:40.737 --> 01:15:42.105
<v BRAXTON>There was four.

861
01:15:42.105 --> 01:15:44.386
Two boys and two girls.

862
01:15:44.386 --> 01:15:53.965
<v DRAGOON>And after your father passed and your mother was working for the garbage collection, were you one of the older, or . . .

863
01:15:53.965 --> 01:15:56.246
<v BRAXTON>I was ten years old.

864
01:15:56.246 --> 01:15:58.070
<v DRAGOON>Oh, you were ten.

865
01:15:58.070 --> 01:16:04.000
So who in the family took the responsibility for childcare and discipline?

866
01:16:04.000 --> 01:16:11.407
<v BRAXTON>Well you know, a great deal of the time we was in school.

867
01:16:11.407 --> 01:16:18.244
Her mother, my oldest sister, was like thirteen years and such older.

868
01:16:18.244 --> 01:16:22.233
So she was grown at the time.

869
01:16:22.233 --> 01:16:31.919
She was her baby, and they lived with us part of the time . . . or very close by.

870
01:16:31.919 --> 01:16:44.453
And honestly, when like I guess if we was out of school, I think we at times we stayed home by ourselves.

871
01:16:44.453 --> 01:16:48.442
<v CARHEEL>A lot of times they took us.

872
01:16:48.442 --> 01:16:53.000
We just went, if they were working.

873
01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:57.722
<v BRAXTON>But a lot of times I think we stayed.

874
01:16:57.722 --> 01:17:06.642
I say that . . . I say I don't know about a lot, but I know like one time,

875
01:17:06.642 --> 01:17:19.235
and I may have mentioned this story before, but I thought my mama was working so hard after my father passed with that job.

876
01:17:19.235 --> 01:17:24.481
She was going to work, something I wasn’t used to.

877
01:17:24.481 --> 01:17:27.630
Somehow I came about some money.

878
01:17:27.630 --> 01:17:38.123
I went to the neighborhood grocery store, Rougeau’s Grocery, and I bought some steak and some rice and some corn.

879
01:17:38.123 --> 01:17:42.321
I came home and I cooked that stuff.

880
01:17:42.321 --> 01:17:50.191
I had never cooked nothing in my life. [Laughs] Or really recall, at this point,

881
01:17:50.191 --> 01:17:59.636
I don’t really recall, I knew a gravy would be dark for some reason, but I don't know.

882
01:17:59.636 --> 01:18:02.259
So I taught myself cooking.

883
01:18:02.259 --> 01:18:10.654
I cooked steak and gravy and rice and corn for my mama when she got home.

884
01:18:10.654 --> 01:18:21.148
I know that never would have happened if it was some other adult in the house at that particular time.

885
01:18:21.148 --> 01:18:27.444
So I think we probably was kind of by ourselves that day.

886
01:18:27.444 --> 01:18:30.068
Even then, it was different.

887
01:18:30.068 --> 01:18:41.086
I guess really, you didn’t have to worry a whole lot about your kids being at home back then by themselves.

888
01:18:41.086 --> 01:18:50.531
It’s just like I was thinking yesterday about like for Halloween, for instance, nowadays people escort their kids.

889
01:18:50.531 --> 01:18:57.352
Not back then, you didn’t have to; you had no worry at all.

890
01:18:57.352 --> 01:19:03.648
The only thing they tell you, maybe, “Watch out for them cars!

891
01:19:03.648 --> 01:19:05.747
Don’t get run over.”

892
01:19:05.747 --> 01:19:14.667
Other than that, the safety of your kids was not even a question in through the neighborhood.

893
01:19:14.667 --> 01:19:20.963
I mean, you just freely roam, went wherever you wanted to go.

894
01:19:20.963 --> 01:19:21.488
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

895
01:19:21.488 --> 01:19:25.685
<v BRAXTON>And you didn’t have to worry about . . . So,

896
01:19:25.685 --> 01:19:37.753
I never had a moment when I was a kid that I was at home and felt threatened or in fear of anything.

897
01:19:37.753 --> 01:19:38.278
Never.

898
01:19:38.278 --> 01:19:40.901
It just never did happen.

899
01:19:40.901 --> 01:19:43.000
It just was . . .

900
01:19:43.000 --> 01:19:46.294
<v DRAGOON>Are you or have you been married?

901
01:19:46.294 --> 01:19:46.765
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

902
01:19:46.765 --> 01:19:48.647
<v DRAGOON>Have you been married?

903
01:19:48.647 --> 01:19:50.529
Or are you currently?

904
01:19:50.529 --> 01:19:51.471
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, yeah.

905
01:19:51.471 --> 01:19:52.412
I’m married.

906
01:19:52.412 --> 01:19:54.294
<v DRAGOON>What’s your spouse’s name?

907
01:19:54.294 --> 01:19:54.765
<v BRAXTON>Giselle.

908
01:19:54.765 --> 01:19:58.059
<v DRAGOON>Is she from the Mossville area, too?

909
01:19:58.059 --> 01:20:00.412
<v BRAXTON>No. No. From Lake Charles.

910
01:20:00.412 --> 01:20:02.294
<v DRAGOON>Oh, from Lake Charles.

911
01:20:02.294 --> 01:20:04.176
Do you have children?

912
01:20:04.176 --> 01:20:04.647
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

913
01:20:04.647 --> 01:20:05.588
<v DRAGOON>How many?

914
01:20:05.588 --> 01:20:06.059
Two?

915
01:20:06.059 --> 01:20:07.000
<v BRAXTON>Two.

916
01:20:07.000 --> 01:20:10.902
<v DRAGOON>And their ages and names?

917
01:20:10.902 --> 01:20:15.585
<v BRAXTON>One is forty-four, forty-five this month.

918
01:20:15.585 --> 01:20:21.049
And the other one is like thirty-five.

919
01:20:21.049 --> 01:20:22.610
<v DRAGOON>Have you . . .

920
01:20:22.610 --> 01:20:23.390
<v BRAXTON>Thirty-seven.

921
01:20:23.390 --> 01:20:39.000
<v DRAGOON>When they were growing up, were you continuing traditions that you . . . traditions that you grew up with, I guess?

922
01:20:39.000 --> 01:20:39.728
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

923
01:20:39.728 --> 01:20:40.457
Sure.

924
01:20:40.457 --> 01:20:51.380
You know, those kind of things are sort of things you kind of do unconsciously.

925
01:20:51.380 --> 01:20:57.207
You do them without really thinking about them.

926
01:20:57.207 --> 01:21:03.761
<v DRAGOON>Did they grow up in the Mossville area, too?

927
01:21:03.761 --> 01:21:04.489
<v BRAXTON>Partially.

928
01:21:04.489 --> 01:21:05.217
<v DRAGOON>Partially?

929
01:21:05.217 --> 01:21:05.946
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

930
01:21:05.946 --> 01:21:20.511
But had a lot of back and forth contact with Mossville, to a great extent. [loud noise] What is that?

931
01:21:20.511 --> 01:21:21.967
A car?

932
01:21:21.967 --> 01:21:24.880
<v CARHEEL>A truck, sounded like.

933
01:21:24.880 --> 01:21:35.076
<v DRAGOON>And growing up, what kinds of activities did you do with your family together?

934
01:21:35.076 --> 01:21:37.989
Like as a family?

935
01:21:37.989 --> 01:21:46.000
I know you mentioned fishing was big in your family.

936
01:21:46.000 --> 01:21:46.515
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

937
01:21:46.515 --> 01:21:47.030
Fishing.

938
01:21:47.030 --> 01:21:53.211
Holidays were a big deal . . . a lot of emphasis put on holidays.

939
01:21:53.211 --> 01:22:00.421
Gardening was a family thing and not so much, like I say, for survival.

940
01:22:00.421 --> 01:22:04.541
It was more of a hobby type thing.

941
01:22:04.541 --> 01:22:10.722
It was something we did for pleasure . . . and raising chickens and such.

942
01:22:10.722 --> 01:22:13.297
It was a family thing.

943
01:22:13.297 --> 01:22:22.053
It wasn’t all about . . . In fact, one time my mother and I was listening to the radio.

944
01:22:22.053 --> 01:22:26.688
That was something we used to do a lot.

945
01:22:26.688 --> 01:22:39.049
You would sit on the porch at night, in particular, and you would turn the radio up and you would listen to the radio.

946
01:22:39.049 --> 01:22:55.530
There was this popular station out of Tennessee that we used to listen to and they used to sell all type of things over the radio by advertisement that you could order.

947
01:22:55.530 --> 01:22:58.620
We heard this ad for chickens.

948
01:22:58.620 --> 01:23:03.771
And we had been talking about trying to have eggs.

949
01:23:03.771 --> 01:23:17.162
I think we might even have mentioned like we could have enough eggs we could sell a few eggs, or whatever, so we decided to order.

950
01:23:17.162 --> 01:23:27.462
It was one hundred chickens and we figured we’d have some chickens, we’d kind of go into the egg business.

951
01:23:27.462 --> 01:23:33.643
If not just for our personal use, but maybe sell a few.

952
01:23:33.643 --> 01:23:41.368
So we got the chickens in good order and everything and we started raising them.

953
01:23:41.368 --> 01:23:49.094
We kept watching them because we were anxious to get started with the egg thing.

954
01:23:49.094 --> 01:23:55.274
And they grew, and they grew, and they grew, and they grew.

955
01:23:55.274 --> 01:24:03.000
All one hundred was roosters. [Laughs] Every single one of them was a rooster.

956
01:24:03.000 --> 01:24:10.216
<v DRAGOON>What did you do about that? [laughs] Did you contact the people you bought them from?

957
01:24:10.216 --> 01:24:12.922
<v BRAXTON>No, they didn’t tell us that.

958
01:24:12.922 --> 01:24:21.039
We just assumed that if you got a hundred biddies, that some of them going to be hens.

959
01:24:21.039 --> 01:24:21.490
Okay?

960
01:24:21.490 --> 01:24:23.294
We just assumed that.

961
01:24:23.294 --> 01:24:30.510
We never would have thought that, but you can, you can tell one chick from another.

962
01:24:30.510 --> 01:24:41.784
You can even, now you can even direct, what’s the word for it, you can actually create a rooster by some means, if you want.

963
01:24:41.784 --> 01:24:49.451
But I don't know if it’s the temperature of the egg when it’s incubating, or it’s something.

964
01:24:49.451 --> 01:24:54.412
But we just knew it was going to be some hens.

965
01:24:54.412 --> 01:24:55.314
How many?

966
01:24:55.314 --> 01:24:56.667
We didn’t know.

967
01:24:56.667 --> 01:25:04.333
But we figured it was going to be out of a hundred, that you’d have some hens.

968
01:25:04.333 --> 01:25:05.235
You know?

969
01:25:05.235 --> 01:25:07.490
Maybe half, maybe a quarter?

970
01:25:07.490 --> 01:25:10.196
But it was one hundred roosters.

971
01:25:10.196 --> 01:25:19.667
I remember a cousin of mine was going to go camping and we was begging for stuff to take to cook.

972
01:25:19.667 --> 01:25:29.588
And my mama told him, “Take one of them damn roosters.” [Laughs] Well, she didn’t say “damn,” because my mama didn’t cuss.

973
01:25:29.588 --> 01:25:33.196
But take one of the roosters, you know.

974
01:25:33.196 --> 01:25:35.000
<v DRAGOON>Oh my goodness.

975
01:25:35.000 --> 01:25:38.911
<v BRAXTON>And we used to butcher the chickens, of course.

976
01:25:38.911 --> 01:25:40.214
<v DRAGOON>Who did that?

977
01:25:40.214 --> 01:25:41.952
Did you do it?

978
01:25:41.952 --> 01:25:42.821
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah.

979
01:25:42.821 --> 01:25:43.256
Yeah.

980
01:25:43.256 --> 01:25:44.125
Oh, yeah.

981
01:25:44.125 --> 01:25:45.429
<v DRAGOON>Everyone knew how?

982
01:25:45.429 --> 01:25:51.946
<v BRAXTON>We had, like the big rooster at the bottom of these oak trees right here,

983
01:25:51.946 --> 01:26:00.202
we used to take that chicken and put his head on that root and take a hatchet and, tap!

984
01:26:00.202 --> 01:26:01.940
Cut his head off.

985
01:26:01.940 --> 01:26:10.196
And believe it or not, they would run around with no head and blood going all over the place.

986
01:26:10.196 --> 01:26:21.494
And whenever they fall and quit kicking, you’d, you know, pluck him and you would take boiled water and you would dip them in boiling water.

987
01:26:21.494 --> 01:26:24.970
That would make the feathers come off easy.

988
01:26:24.970 --> 01:26:29.315
Because you had to pick the feathers off by hand.

989
01:26:29.315 --> 01:26:31.488
They don’t do that now.

990
01:26:31.488 --> 01:26:34.095
They put them in a machine.

991
01:26:34.095 --> 01:26:36.702
<v CARHEEL>[Laughs] You should see her face.

992
01:26:36.702 --> 01:26:41.482
<v DRAGOON>Did everyone know how to butcher the chickens, or was that . . .

993
01:26:41.482 --> 01:26:42.351
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah.

994
01:26:42.351 --> 01:26:44.089
That was pretty much . . .

995
01:26:44.089 --> 01:26:48.000
<v DRAGOON>. . . or that was something just the boys did?

996
01:26:48.000 --> 01:26:51.264
<v BRAXTON>That was pretty much like common knowledge.

997
01:26:51.264 --> 01:26:53.596
That was something you did.

998
01:26:53.596 --> 01:26:56.393
In fact, you know, say common,

999
01:26:56.393 --> 01:27:06.185
like my mother told me the story about when she was a little girl and her daddy would kill chicken hawks,

1000
01:27:06.185 --> 01:27:08.983
and used to eat chicken hawks.

1001
01:27:08.983 --> 01:27:15.978
Her chore was to clean them and she had done that a number of times.

1002
01:27:15.978 --> 01:27:27.169
She was cleaning these chicken hawks this one time, and when she cut them open and gutting them, some, the chicken hawks had eaten

1003
01:27:27.169 --> 01:27:33.697
<c.p>some live baby snakes, and they were still alive when she cut it open.

1004
01:27:33.697 --> 01:27:36.961
And that just did it for her.

1005
01:27:36.961 --> 01:27:42.090
I mean, that was a little more than she could handle.

1006
01:27:42.090 --> 01:27:52.815
So I never did know, you know, after that, I’m sure on that particular occasion, her dad probably finished it up, I imagine.

1007
01:27:52.815 --> 01:27:59.343
But I never knew whether she ever cleaned any chicken hawks after that again.

1008
01:27:59.343 --> 01:28:02.140
But chickens, oh, yeah, no doubt.

1009
01:28:02.140 --> 01:28:07.270
That was a common thing that people knew how to do.

1010
01:28:07.270 --> 01:28:08.202
<v CARHEEL>Chicken, fish.

1011
01:28:08.202 --> 01:28:08.669
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

1012
01:28:08.669 --> 01:28:10.067
Fish and chicken.

1013
01:28:10.067 --> 01:28:11.000
<v CARHEEL>Crab.

1014
01:28:11.000 --> 01:28:12.397
<v BRAXTON>And crabs.

1015
01:28:12.397 --> 01:28:25.667
Now men probably spent more time cleaning game animals, like squirrels and coons and possums and all that stuff.

1016
01:28:25.667 --> 01:28:35.444
But you know, chickens and fish and crab, everybody knew how to do that.

1017
01:28:35.444 --> 01:28:44.524
<v DRAGOON>What kind of fruits and vegetables did you guys have in your garden?

1018
01:28:44.524 --> 01:28:45.222
<v BRAXTON>Everything.

1019
01:28:45.222 --> 01:28:45.921
<v DRAGOON>Everything?

1020
01:28:45.921 --> 01:28:47.317
<v BRAXTON>No, seriously.

1021
01:28:47.317 --> 01:28:53.603
I mean, greens, peas, and beans . . . corn, peppers, strawberries.

1022
01:28:53.603 --> 01:28:55.000
<v CARHEEL>Peaches.

1023
01:28:55.000 --> 01:29:04.829
<v BRAXTON>We had . . . I mean, one time when Mother planted a whole lot of things, you know.

1024
01:29:04.829 --> 01:29:05.443
<v CARHEEL>Oranges.

1025
01:29:05.443 --> 01:29:06.057
Lemons.

1026
01:29:06.057 --> 01:29:09.129
And all kinds of nuts.

1027
01:29:09.129 --> 01:29:09.743
<v DRAGOON>Everything.

1028
01:29:09.743 --> 01:29:12.814
It really sounds like everything.

1029
01:29:12.814 --> 01:29:13.429
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

1030
01:29:13.429 --> 01:29:17.114
All kind of variety of things.

1031
01:29:17.114 --> 01:29:19.571
Over time, you know.

1032
01:29:19.571 --> 01:29:23.871
We used to grow watermelon sometimes . . . cantaloupes.

1033
01:29:23.871 --> 01:29:33.700
It wasn’t quite the right kind of soil to support watermelons, but we used to try.

1034
01:29:33.700 --> 01:29:38.000
We used to do it sometimes.

1035
01:29:38.000 --> 01:29:49.389
<v DRAGOON>I want to ask a few questions more about like medical treatment and where you guys went to the doctor growing up.

1036
01:29:49.389 --> 01:29:54.049
Did you have to leave Mossville to get treatment?

1037
01:29:54.049 --> 01:29:59.743
You talked about there were a couple of healers around here.

1038
01:29:59.743 --> 01:30:06.473
<v BRAXTON>There were no formal medical doctors and medical people in the Mossville area.

1039
01:30:06.473 --> 01:30:12.686
So we used, during the period of time I was coming up,

1040
01:30:12.686 --> 01:30:22.522
the doctors we went to were in Maplewood, which was like three or four miles from Mossville by road.

1041
01:30:22.522 --> 01:30:27.699
And Cal Cam Hospital in Sulphur, it was in Sulphur.

1042
01:30:27.699 --> 01:30:37.018
Our family doctor when we were growing up, for the most part, was a Doctor Fellows in Sulphur.

1043
01:30:37.018 --> 01:30:49.960
And I just recently found out all my life, some way, somehow, I thought I was born in Sulphur, Louisiana, until about a month ago.

1044
01:30:49.960 --> 01:30:52.549
I went trying to retrieve . . .

1045
01:30:52.549 --> 01:30:56.173
<c.p>I went and bought some birth certificates.

1046
01:30:56.173 --> 01:31:06.527
I had my birth certificate, but the one I got years ago, I just got the little small wallet size.

1047
01:31:06.527 --> 01:31:09.115
Not much information on it.

1048
01:31:09.115 --> 01:31:18.951
So I decided I was going to go buy the full-sized birth certificate that had more information on it.

1049
01:31:18.951 --> 01:31:27.235
And when I got it, it had on there that I was born in Lake Charles.

1050
01:31:27.235 --> 01:31:30.858
And my doctor was a Doctor Price.

1051
01:31:30.858 --> 01:31:35.000
I was born in Saint Patrick Hospital.

1052
01:31:35.000 --> 01:31:40.204
That took me back a bit, you know, to find that out.

1053
01:31:40.204 --> 01:31:44.541
And I told the lady, I say, “This ain’t me.

1054
01:31:44.541 --> 01:31:45.842
This ain’t mine.”

1055
01:31:45.842 --> 01:31:53.214
Then she called me to the counter and she said, “I’m not supposed to show you this,

1056
01:31:53.214 --> 01:31:58.418
you can’t hold it, but I’m going to let you see it.”

1057
01:31:58.418 --> 01:32:06.658
And it was the actual copy that she was looking at that she made the official birth certificate from.

1058
01:32:06.658 --> 01:32:17.500
It stated on there all the information that was on the copy she gave me, plus a lot of other information, including my mother’s signature.

1059
01:32:17.500 --> 01:32:21.403
Her signature, I’ve been seeing it all my life.

1060
01:32:21.403 --> 01:32:24.872
I know her signature when I see it.

1061
01:32:24.872 --> 01:32:27.041
That’s the way it was.

1062
01:32:27.041 --> 01:32:37.015
But somehow, I thought I was born in Sulphur, and that my birth doctor was the same doctor that was our family doctor.

1063
01:32:37.015 --> 01:32:42.219
I can remember when I was five, he was our family doctor.

1064
01:32:42.219 --> 01:32:51.327
But he was not my birth doctor, and I was not born at the hospital that he worked at in Sulphur.

1065
01:32:51.327 --> 01:32:53.929
So that was quite an awakening.

1066
01:32:53.929 --> 01:33:00.000
<v DRAGOON>Were most births . . . did women mostly go to Sulphur Hospital to give birth?

1067
01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:01.143
<v BRAXTON>Pretty much.

1068
01:33:01.143 --> 01:33:13.714
During the time I was growing up, midwife births, the area we live in, was kind of a thing of the past.

1069
01:33:13.714 --> 01:33:17.714
We had hospitals that was close by.

1070
01:33:17.714 --> 01:33:24.000
<v DRAGOON>Was it maybe your parents’ generation that did midwife births?

1071
01:33:24.000 --> 01:33:24.935
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, right.

1072
01:33:24.935 --> 01:33:28.675
There was a lot more of that then.

1073
01:33:28.675 --> 01:33:34.286
In fact . . . and this is something we just never really talked about,

1074
01:33:34.286 --> 01:33:39.896
but her mother, my oldest sister, was probably born by the midwife.

1075
01:33:39.896 --> 01:33:45.039
In fact, I could just about bet on it, you know.

1076
01:33:45.039 --> 01:33:52.987
Because I found, doing my ancestry search, when her mother was five and a half months old,

1077
01:33:52.987 --> 01:34:00.468
exactly where they was staying at and who was living in the house at the time.

1078
01:34:00.468 --> 01:34:07.948
It was her mother, my mother and father, my father’s brother and my father’s other brother.

1079
01:34:07.948 --> 01:34:10.286
There were two of them.

1080
01:34:10.286 --> 01:34:13.558
But my father was head of house.

1081
01:34:13.558 --> 01:34:18.701
The house that they was living in was the ancestral home.

1082
01:34:18.701 --> 01:34:21.506
But he was head of house.

1083
01:34:21.506 --> 01:34:28.987
Actually, one of his brothers was older than him, but he was still head of house.

1084
01:34:28.987 --> 01:34:30.857
<v DRAGOON>How was that decided?

1085
01:34:30.857 --> 01:34:33.195
Or how did that happen?

1086
01:34:33.195 --> 01:34:35.065
<v BRAXTON>How did that happen?

1087
01:34:35.065 --> 01:34:36.000
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

1088
01:34:36.000 --> 01:34:40.399
<v BRAXTON>Oh, you did have to ask that.

1089
01:34:40.399 --> 01:34:44.798
There was a reason that that happened.

1090
01:34:44.798 --> 01:34:56.738
The same year that her mother was born, my father’s oldest brother basically was released from incarceration for murder.

1091
01:34:56.738 --> 01:35:00.508
But he . . . well, manslaughter, not murder.

1092
01:35:00.508 --> 01:35:02.393
It was manslaughter.

1093
01:35:02.393 --> 01:35:12.448
But his sentence was suspended because at the time it occurred, he was seventeen years old.

1094
01:35:12.448 --> 01:35:23.760
But on that particular day, when her mother was five and a half months old, he was forty-three.

1095
01:35:23.760 --> 01:35:34.443
So what transpired was, when he committed the murder, the manslaughter, when he was seventeen, he ran.

1096
01:35:34.443 --> 01:35:38.213
And of course the guy died.

1097
01:35:38.213 --> 01:35:44.497
He hit the guy in the head with a jug.

1098
01:35:44.497 --> 01:35:52.038
Like a Granny jug from the Beverly Hillbillies, those heavy ceramic jugs?

1099
01:35:52.038 --> 01:35:54.552
And the guy died.

1100
01:35:54.552 --> 01:35:56.437
And he ran.

1101
01:35:56.437 --> 01:35:57.694
And they

1102
01:35:57.694 --> 01:36:00.836
<c.p>caught him twenty-five years later.

1103
01:36:00.836 --> 01:36:13.404
So when they caught him twenty-five years later in Houston, they, he was in jail for a month or two.

1104
01:36:13.404 --> 01:36:22.202
They had his trial and they turned him loose because of some extenuating circumstances.

1105
01:36:22.202 --> 01:36:26.601
They turned him loose on suspended sentence.

1106
01:36:26.601 --> 01:36:31.000
And he ended up living [out?].

1107
01:36:31.000 --> 01:36:34.949
<v DRAGOON>And he was the oldest brother?

1108
01:36:34.949 --> 01:36:43.506
<v BRAXTON>He wasn’t the oldest brother, but he was older than my father was.

1109
01:36:43.506 --> 01:36:57.329
The oldest brother was my Uncle Rogers on that side of the family, who was a barber in the area here.

1110
01:36:57.329 --> 01:37:05.228
He actually was a barber in Mossville for a period of time.

1111
01:37:05.228 --> 01:37:09.835
<v DRAGOON>But he didn’t live in the house?

1112
01:37:09.835 --> 01:37:17.734
<v BRAXTON>No, he was married and had his own place in Lake Charles.

1113
01:37:17.734 --> 01:37:23.000
<v DRAGOON>Did the doctors ever make house calls?

1114
01:37:23.000 --> 01:37:24.359
<v BRAXTON>They did.

1115
01:37:24.359 --> 01:37:33.875
When, for various reasons, I can recall several different doctors coming to our home.

1116
01:37:33.875 --> 01:37:46.789
Specifically, I clearly remember that my father had surgery in his, he was in the hospital for two weeks.

1117
01:37:46.789 --> 01:37:51.547
His condition was determined to be terminal.

1118
01:37:51.547 --> 01:37:59.703
So he came home and he lived about two weeks at home.

1119
01:37:59.703 --> 01:38:13.977
And during that period of time at home, the doctor would come to the house on a number of different occasions.

1120
01:38:13.977 --> 01:38:20.773
There was another time where she was sick, she thought.

1121
01:38:20.773 --> 01:38:31.648
It was thought that she had diphtheria, but it proved not to be the case later

1122
01:38:31.648 --> 01:38:32.328
<c.p>on.

1123
01:38:32.328 --> 01:38:35.727
The whole family was quarantined.

1124
01:38:35.727 --> 01:38:43.883
The only family I know in my whole life that was quarantined.

1125
01:38:43.883 --> 01:38:50.000
I don't know nobody that was quarantined. [Laughs]

1126
01:38:50.000 --> 01:38:51.610
<v DRAGOON>Who quarantined you?

1127
01:38:51.610 --> 01:38:53.756
How did that happen?

1128
01:38:53.756 --> 01:39:05.561
<v BRAXTON>Because when she went to the doctor, the health department said hey, we think you have a very serious, transmittable disease, diphtheria.

1129
01:39:05.561 --> 01:39:08.780
You probably never heard of it.

1130
01:39:08.780 --> 01:39:12.537
But it used to be, it’s deadly.

1131
01:39:12.537 --> 01:39:17.902
And all inside your mouth would turn white and everything.

1132
01:39:17.902 --> 01:39:21.122
Back of your throat and such.

1133
01:39:21.122 --> 01:39:30.780
And I don't think nobody else actually ended up having it, or signs of it, besides just her.

1134
01:39:30.780 --> 01:39:38.829
But you know, and so the health department, we was quarantined under strict quarantine orders.

1135
01:39:38.829 --> 01:39:41.512
<v DRAGOON>You couldn’t leave the house?

1136
01:39:41.512 --> 01:39:44.195
<v BRAXTON>No. Couldn’t leave the . . . premise.

1137
01:39:44.195 --> 01:39:46.878
Nobody could come in, neither.

1138
01:39:46.878 --> 01:39:48.488
<v DRAGOON>For how long?

1139
01:39:48.488 --> 01:39:50.634
<v BRAXTON>I don’t really know.

1140
01:39:50.634 --> 01:39:53.854
It was a good while, though.

1141
01:39:53.854 --> 01:39:56.000
<v DRAGOON>That sounds scary.

1142
01:39:56.000 --> 01:40:06.698
<v BRAXTON>It was like probably a couple, several weeks, I would guess, or something of that order.

1143
01:40:06.698 --> 01:40:08.035
You know?

1144
01:40:08.035 --> 01:40:12.715
That was just the way it was.

1145
01:40:12.715 --> 01:40:26.756
But she obviously recovered . . . I think. [Laughs] So I recall doctors and some nurses and some stuff coming at that point.

1146
01:40:26.756 --> 01:40:36.785
But even outside that, you know, doctors would occasionally make house calls for some people.

1147
01:40:36.785 --> 01:40:44.140
Not so much as a routine but during that same period,

1148
01:40:44.140 --> 01:40:52.831
people used to have funerals, have their wakes at home, too . . . deceased people.

1149
01:40:52.831 --> 01:41:06.872
A number of wakes and stuff that was held, I can, my grandfather, I think it was . . . 1950-something when he died.

1150
01:41:06.872 --> 01:41:15.564
I can remember the wake, having a wake across the street from us.

1151
01:41:15.564 --> 01:41:23.587
That’s where they lived, right across the street . . . at home and everything.

1152
01:41:23.587 --> 01:41:28.936
That was a common thing then . . . real common.

1153
01:41:28.936 --> 01:41:34.285
Then it just, it went out real quick.

1154
01:41:34.285 --> 01:41:39.634
Like it just stopped all of a sudden.

1155
01:41:39.634 --> 01:41:46.320
<v CARHEEL>The last one that I remember was when Grandma died.

1156
01:41:46.320 --> 01:41:49.663
Hers was at her house.

1157
01:41:49.663 --> 01:41:51.000
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

1158
01:41:51.000 --> 01:41:52.957
<v CARHEEL>That was ’73?

1159
01:41:52.957 --> 01:41:53.609
<v BRAXTON>1973.

1160
01:41:53.609 --> 01:41:54.261
<v CARHEEL>’73?

1161
01:41:54.261 --> 01:41:54.913
Yeah.

1162
01:41:54.913 --> 01:41:59.478
<v BRAXTON>That’s the last one I remember, too.

1163
01:41:59.478 --> 01:42:06.000
<v CARHEEL>That’s the last one I remember in Mossville, period.

1164
01:42:06.000 --> 01:42:18.562
<v BRAXTON>And that was just simply because the Towner family kind of like old-time traditional thing,

1165
01:42:18.562 --> 01:42:29.450
as a whole, it just kind of runs through the family like that.

1166
01:42:29.450 --> 01:42:42.850
So the last one, that was after my grandmother on my mother’s side, Ivy Viola Towner.

1167
01:42:42.850 --> 01:42:47.038
<v CARHEEL>She had, what, thirteen children?

1168
01:42:47.038 --> 01:42:49.550
Twelve or thirteen?

1169
01:42:49.550 --> 01:42:52.062
How many children?

1170
01:42:52.062 --> 01:42:54.575
<v BRAXTON>Something like that.

1171
01:42:54.575 --> 01:42:55.412
<v CARHEEL>Fifty . . .

1172
01:42:55.412 --> 01:42:56.250
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

1173
01:42:56.250 --> 01:43:00.438
<v CARHEEL>Fifty-four grandchildren and 113 great-grandchildren.

1174
01:43:00.438 --> 01:43:04.625
I don't remember after that.

1175
01:43:04.625 --> 01:43:13.000
I was in that great-grandchildren number, I remember that.

1176
01:43:13.000 --> 01:43:13.759
<v DRAGOON>Alright.

1177
01:43:13.759 --> 01:43:24.389
Well I’m going to go on into a kind of different set of questions.

1178
01:43:24.389 --> 01:43:29.704
What are the geographic boundaries of Mossville?

1179
01:43:29.704 --> 01:43:31.981
Can you explain?

1180
01:43:31.981 --> 01:43:34.259
<v BRAXTON>The geographic boundaries?

1181
01:43:34.259 --> 01:43:50.963
Well, Mossville, for the most part, extends on the north and the south sides of the Old Spanish Trail from ConocoPhillips now.

1182
01:43:50.963 --> 01:44:07.667
That plant was built there in the ‘40s and it was built right up against the Mossville neighborhood where it started at.

1183
01:44:07.667 --> 01:44:09.944
<c.p>It was rural.

1184
01:44:09.944 --> 01:44:28.167
So from ConocoPhillips to the east, all the way to basically the Sulphur, what’s the Sulphur city limits now, I believe, to the west.

1185
01:44:28.167 --> 01:44:41.074
Then to the south, it extended from the Old Spanish Trail down Prater Road to Highway 90.

1186
01:44:41.074 --> 01:44:58.537
Then to the north, it extended from the Old Spanish Trail down Evergreen Road to approximately a mile and something in that direction.

1187
01:44:58.537 --> 01:45:16.000
But along Old Spanish Trail alone, Mossville spanned a distance of about two and a half miles, or thereabouts, east to west.

1188
01:45:16.000 --> 01:45:25.322
<v DRAGOON>Do you know how the community of Mossville reacted to ConocoPhillips being built in the ‘40s?

1189
01:45:25.322 --> 01:45:38.722
<v BRAXTON>When it was built in the ‘40s, I think that it was probably a positive reaction because it was a very small facility.

1190
01:45:38.722 --> 01:45:51.539
It pretty much remained that way, very small, all the way up until the point in time that I actually worked there.

1191
01:45:51.539 --> 01:45:56.200
That’s where I worked at for forty years.

1192
01:45:56.200 --> 01:46:12.513
So I actually had the opportunity to see it expand from what was nearly its original wartime size back when it was built around the World War Two.

1193
01:46:12.513 --> 01:46:18.339
It was kind of built around supporting the war effort.

1194
01:46:18.339 --> 01:46:23.000
<v DRAGOON>What was your experience like working there?

1195
01:46:23.000 --> 01:46:25.965
<v BRAXTON>It was a good experience.

1196
01:46:25.965 --> 01:46:28.930
I’ve seen things change dramatically.

1197
01:46:28.930 --> 01:46:40.791
To some extent, things change because, especially when you talk about environmental issues, a lot of that was politically driven.

1198
01:46:40.791 --> 01:46:54.430
But I’m also glad that outside of that, there were things that happened that I played a role in as far as improvement.

1199
01:46:54.430 --> 01:46:58.581
If it was nothing else except the

1200
01:46:58.581 --> 01:47:19.930
<c.p>consciousness and the personal reactions of some of the people toward some of the things that we could do about that didn’t necessarily cost a whole lot of money and so forth that made a difference.

1201
01:47:19.930 --> 01:47:22.302
So things change dramatically.

1202
01:47:22.302 --> 01:47:31.198
The air is much cleaner now with this plant being as large as it is,

1203
01:47:31.198 --> 01:47:38.907
the air is cleaner than it was when it was very, very small.

1204
01:47:38.907 --> 01:47:41.279
So things have improved.

1205
01:47:41.279 --> 01:47:51.953
A plant like that is a necessary evil for the day and the society that we live in.

1206
01:47:51.953 --> 01:48:05.000
It’s just that we have to manage them in the best way that we can to support the environment and such.

1207
01:48:05.000 --> 01:48:14.241
<v DRAGOON>When you say you played a role in making a difference, what do you mean by that?

1208
01:48:14.241 --> 01:48:21.851
<v BRAXTON>I worked in management, so I was able to speak up and say hey,

1209
01:48:21.851 --> 01:48:30.548
you know, we need to improve the way we communicate this or that to the public.

1210
01:48:30.548 --> 01:48:33.266
I even had an opportunity,

1211
01:48:33.266 --> 01:48:45.768
I was chosen to write how we go about interfacing with the community around events that we have take place inside the plant.

1212
01:48:45.768 --> 01:48:51.747
So I had this exhaustive flow chart that was put together.

1213
01:48:51.747 --> 01:48:55.008
If this happens, you do this,

1214
01:48:55.008 --> 01:49:04.793
just . . . and follow this flow chart so that everybody repeated things in the same way all the time.

1215
01:49:04.793 --> 01:49:06.423
It was structured.

1216
01:49:06.423 --> 01:49:12.402
It was not just anybody do whatever they want to do.

1217
01:49:12.402 --> 01:49:25.448
That piece was combined with not just only, it was three pieces of it to a great, a more, it was who you notify,

1218
01:49:25.448 --> 01:49:38.494
whether it’s the community, different government agencies, the employees and such at the plant, and what response the people in the plant should take,

1219
01:49:38.494 --> 01:49:45.560
what response, based on what’s happening, the people in the community should take,

1220
01:49:45.560 --> 01:49:59.693
who in the community we should notify to make sure that that happens, if we had, say, for instance, some kind of gas release or whatever.

1221
01:49:59.693 --> 01:50:06.216
So the very first system we had, I put it together personally.

1222
01:50:06.216 --> 01:50:10.564
Basically it’s still being used at this time.

1223
01:50:10.564 --> 01:50:16.000
And that was like in 1990 or something thereabouts.

1224
01:50:16.000 --> 01:50:16.319
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

1225
01:50:16.319 --> 01:50:19.830
So it sounds like you’re a kind of community liaison, almost?

1226
01:50:19.830 --> 01:50:20.787
<v BRAXTON>No, not me.

1227
01:50:20.787 --> 01:50:22.702
I never wanted that role. [Laughs]

1228
01:50:22.702 --> 01:50:24.617
<v DRAGOON>But they gave it to you?

1229
01:50:24.617 --> 01:50:25.574
<v BRAXTON>No. I wasn’t.

1230
01:50:25.574 --> 01:50:26.851
I wrote the script.

1231
01:50:26.851 --> 01:50:29.085
I wasn’t necessarily, I wrote it for . . .

1232
01:50:29.085 --> 01:50:31.000
<v DRAGOON>You were behind the scenes?

1233
01:50:31.000 --> 01:50:35.084
<v BRAXTON>I wrote it for others to use, okay?

1234
01:50:35.084 --> 01:50:39.678
I mean, sometimes that other was me, you know.

1235
01:50:39.678 --> 01:50:50.399
But no, I wasn’t, I mean, I served in that purpose at times, because I was the person that ended up,

1236
01:50:50.399 --> 01:51:00.608
if we had a bad fire or explosion or bad gas release, well, I was in charge of the refinery.

1237
01:51:00.608 --> 01:51:05.713
I would be the one responsible for making these calls.

1238
01:51:05.713 --> 01:51:13.371
Depending on the situation you would call different people, notify different people about different things.

1239
01:51:13.371 --> 01:51:19.497
But we haven’t had any events of that nature occur around here.

1240
01:51:19.497 --> 01:51:29.706
Things have improved dramatically. [speaking to Carheel] Where was you at when that real big explosion went off at Conoco?

1241
01:51:29.706 --> 01:51:32.259
Where you all was at?

1242
01:51:32.259 --> 01:51:36.853
I’m trying to remember where you all was at.

1243
01:51:36.853 --> 01:51:38.385
<v CARHEEL>I was on . . .

1244
01:51:38.385 --> 01:51:39.406
<v BRAXTON>In ’85.

1245
01:51:39.406 --> 01:51:40.427
<v CARHEEL>. . . Sulphur Avenue.

1246
01:51:40.427 --> 01:51:40.937
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

1247
01:51:40.937 --> 01:51:44.000
<v CARHEEL>I was on Sulphur Avenue.

1248
01:51:44.000 --> 01:51:45.338
<v BRAXTON>I thought so.

1249
01:51:45.338 --> 01:51:46.676
That’s an example.

1250
01:51:46.676 --> 01:51:48.014
She remembered that.

1251
01:51:48.014 --> 01:51:52.919
It was . . . It knocked out windows all around in the area.

1252
01:51:52.919 --> 01:51:55.149
It was really powerful thing.

1253
01:51:55.149 --> 01:52:05.405
I know she remembered that one particular thing because it showed up on the Richter scale for earthquakes and such, you know? [Laughs]

1254
01:52:05.405 --> 01:52:07.189
<v CARHEEL>Yeah, I remember that.

1255
01:52:07.189 --> 01:52:13.878
<v BRAXTON>Well I was in charge of the plant that morning and I know, I say . . .

1256
01:52:13.878 --> 01:52:17.000
<v CARHEEL>I heard it when it happened.

1257
01:52:17.000 --> 01:52:19.304
<v BRAXTON>It’s 5:20 in the morning.

1258
01:52:19.304 --> 01:52:23.912
I said . . . I know they just all thinking I’m dead.

1259
01:52:23.912 --> 01:52:30.363
I know they all knew because it woke the whole city up, the area.

1260
01:52:30.363 --> 01:52:33.588
So I called one of them, somebody.

1261
01:52:33.588 --> 01:52:40.500
I don't know, I think I called her mama and told her to tell every . . .

1262
01:52:40.500 --> 01:52:41.882
<v CARHEEL>I don't know.

1263
01:52:41.882 --> 01:52:47.873
I just remember I went to your house to see about your girls.

1264
01:52:47.873 --> 01:52:49.716
To check on them.

1265
01:52:49.716 --> 01:52:53.402
<v BRAXTON>Oh, no. That was, that was another time.

1266
01:52:53.402 --> 01:52:59.853
No, that was . . . yeah, that was another plant that blew up there, I believe.

1267
01:52:59.853 --> 01:53:04.000
<v CARHEEL>No. This was when Conoco blew up, right?

1268
01:53:04.000 --> 01:53:05.113
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah?

1269
01:53:05.113 --> 01:53:06.783
It could be.

1270
01:53:06.783 --> 01:53:08.453
I don't remember.

1271
01:53:08.453 --> 01:53:11.236
It’s been several of them.

1272
01:53:11.236 --> 01:53:17.915
But yeah, that was . . . So I had the opportunity to be involved.

1273
01:53:17.915 --> 01:53:30.160
From day to day I had opportunity to work on improving things in my area about what we would call fugitive emissions,

1274
01:53:30.160 --> 01:53:39.066
emissions of different gaseous and liquid chemicals that we really should be keeping inside the pipes,

1275
01:53:39.066 --> 01:53:46.858
but they might be leaking out into the atmosphere . . . like benzene and so forth.

1276
01:53:46.858 --> 01:53:57.434
So yeah, I had my role and my part that I was able to personally inject into improving things.

1277
01:53:57.434 --> 01:54:03.000
<v CARHEEL>And you initiated the crossing light and the crossing . . .

1278
01:54:03.000 --> 01:54:09.233
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, I did that, too when you go through the Old Spanish Trail.

1279
01:54:09.233 --> 01:54:16.904
I kept advocating that somebody was going to get run over crossing the street and such,

1280
01:54:16.904 --> 01:54:30.808
and that we needed to have a caution light to help with the traffic flow and the pedestrian flow . . . and a reduction in the speed limit through the area.

1281
01:54:30.808 --> 01:54:33.205
<v CARHEEL>Yeah, because it was pretty . . .

1282
01:54:33.205 --> 01:54:33.685
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

1283
01:54:33.685 --> 01:54:38.000
<v CARHEEL>I said yeah, because it was pretty fast.

1284
01:54:38.000 --> 01:54:38.593
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

1285
01:54:38.593 --> 01:54:42.148
So, I mean, that was self-satisfying.

1286
01:54:42.148 --> 01:54:44.519
I enjoyed my job.

1287
01:54:44.519 --> 01:54:49.852
I quit at the top of my game. [Laughs]

1288
01:54:49.852 --> 01:54:54.593
<v DRAGOON>So we’re about at two hours right now.

1289
01:54:54.593 --> 01:55:01.704
I would like to take a quick restroom break, if that’s okay.

1290
01:55:01.704 --> 01:55:02.296
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

1291
01:55:02.296 --> 01:55:10.000
<v DRAGOON>And then maybe we could talk about continuing for a little longer.

1292
01:55:10.000 --> 01:55:10.536
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

1293
01:55:10.536 --> 01:55:23.942
And you had asked a question earlier about decision making and such in the home that I kind of glanced over it a little bit.

1294
01:55:23.942 --> 01:55:26.623
I was thinking about it.

1295
01:55:26.623 --> 01:55:27.159
<v DRAGOON>Yeah.

1296
01:55:27.159 --> 01:55:32.522
<c.p>[COOPER?]: So maybe we can come back to that? [pause]

1297
01:55:32.522 --> 01:55:33.058
<v DRAGOON>Alright.

1298
01:55:33.058 --> 01:55:36.275
We’re back from our mini break.

1299
01:55:36.275 --> 01:55:47.000
We just had another question that we wanted to revisit, which was how decisions were made in your family.

1300
01:55:47.000 --> 01:55:54.988
<v BRAXTON>I’m still wrestling with exactly how to answer that,

1301
01:55:54.988 --> 01:56:16.288
because I came up from the age of ten without a father in the household, but my mother was the head of the household.

1302
01:56:16.288 --> 01:56:37.588
I can say that my mother was . . . she expected us to be well disciplined and discipline was something she drove with us every day.

1303
01:56:37.588 --> 01:56:57.112
It was basically by talking and assuring that we had a good understanding of what was expected of us around certain things.

1304
01:56:57.112 --> 01:57:00.662
She made the decisions.

1305
01:57:00.662 --> 01:57:20.188
My mother made the decisions around most things that affected our life—not just discipline, but we didn’t just go somewhere without inquiring,

1306
01:57:20.188 --> 01:57:37.050
asking, and having agreement from her on what we could do or go, and how long we could stay.

1307
01:57:37.050 --> 01:57:55.688
I can remember times when I attempted in my mannish ways to go astray from some of those decisions and such.

1308
01:57:55.688 --> 01:58:01.012
I learned my lessons from them.

1309
01:58:01.012 --> 01:58:09.000
But she was good at giving us guidance.

1310
01:58:09.000 --> 01:58:16.595
During those times, the decisions about what you did was based a lot on lessons learned,

1311
01:58:16.595 --> 01:58:25.139
that you learned, and you learned a lot—a lot more than what kids learn today, in my mind.

1312
01:58:25.139 --> 01:58:27.038
It was substantially different.

1313
01:58:27.038 --> 01:58:30.835
It’s hard to put your finger on it,

1314
01:58:30.835 --> 01:58:43.177
but it was a lot more discussion and talk going on in the family around things that happen, whether they were good things or bad things.

1315
01:58:43.177 --> 01:58:49.348
And you learned a lot more from it than tends to happen today.

1316
01:58:49.348 --> 01:58:55.994
So I guess it’s hard for me to answer that question, to some extent,

1317
01:58:55.994 --> 01:59:05.487
because it seems like when I think about it, being that I came up in a fatherless home from ten,

1318
01:59:05.487 --> 01:59:17.829
all the decisions were my mother’s, for the most part, which is maybe somewhat different from a lot of others because that responsibility was on her.

1319
01:59:17.829 --> 01:59:25.899
I remember there were a lot of things as I was coming up, I just felt obligated,

1320
01:59:25.899 --> 01:59:33.019
even as a teen, to take on the role of the man to some extent.

1321
01:59:33.019 --> 01:59:36.342
That’s how I ended up started working.

1322
01:59:36.342 --> 01:59:45.361
Because I just felt that I was supposed to be part of the, being the breadwinner in this house.

1323
01:59:45.361 --> 01:59:52.956
And when I went to work, I can tell you the first job that I got.

1324
01:59:52.956 --> 01:59:56.753
My mother never, ever asked me for a

1325
01:59:56.753 --> 02:00:00.551
<c.p>penny of anything that I ever worked for.

1326
02:00:00.551 --> 02:00:02.924
But I just felt obligated.

1327
02:00:02.924 --> 02:00:12.892
It was automatically, that’s what I was supposed to do was to go to work and help provide for the family.

1328
02:00:12.892 --> 02:00:15.741
So I just did that automatically.

1329
02:00:15.741 --> 02:00:20.487
It just was . . . it wasn’t even anything to think about.

1330
02:00:20.487 --> 02:00:25.709
That’s pretty much it I can think about, related to that.

1331
02:00:25.709 --> 02:00:26.184
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1332
02:00:26.184 --> 02:00:30.930
I know you mentioned you all had to get going.

1333
02:00:30.930 --> 02:00:39.000
Thank you so much for doing the second session with us today and sharing your stories.

1334
02:00:39.000 --> 02:00:39.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4524.

1335
02:00:39.429 --> 02:00:39.857
End Session II.]
